DTSTART;TZID=Europe/Berlin:20240603T170000
DTEND;TZID=Europe/Berlin:20240603T180000
DTSTAMP:20260325T005103Z
ORGANIZER;CN=vdujn2pbpkhgr5f55cm39qbmu8@group.calendar.google.com:mailto:vd
 ujn2pbpkhgr5f55cm39qbmu8@group.calendar.google.com
UID:4om8dkr7eu2gvvk4o4mi4c9bjg_R20231211T160000@google.com
ATTENDEE;CUTYPE=INDIVIDUAL;ROLE=REQ-PARTICIPANT;PARTSTAT=NEEDS-ACTION;CN=be
 njamin.murrell@ki.se;X-NUM-GUESTS=0:mailto:benjamin.murrell@ki.se
RECURRENCE-ID;TZID=Europe/Berlin:20240603T170000
CREATED:20210724T162031Z
DESCRIPTION:<u></u><u></u><u></u><u></u><u></u><b>Speaker:<br></b>Ben Murre
 ll. The Murrell lab works on various topics related to Computational Biolog
 y\,<br>Virology\, and Immunology. The lab's current focus is on questions r
 elated to<br>SARS-CoV-2/COVID-19 vaccines and therapeutics.<br><br><b>Paper
 :</b><br><b>The Continuous Language of Protein Structure </b><a href="https
 ://www.biorxiv.org/content/10.1101/2024.05.11.593685v1">https://www.biorxiv
 .org/content/10.1101/2024.05.11.593685v1</a> (Lukas Billera\, Anton Oresten
 \, Aron Stålmarck\, Kenta Sato\, Mateusz Kaduk\, Ben Murrell)<br>Just as la
 nguage is composed of sublexical tokens that combine to form words\, senten
 ces\, and paragraphs\, protein backbones are composed of sub-structural ele
 ments that combine to form helices\, sheets\, folds\, domains\, and chains.
  Autoregressive language models operate on discrete tokens\, whereas protei
 n structure is inherently continuous\, and generative approaches to protein
  design have borrowed more from image generation than language modeling. Bu
 t autoregressive models do not inherently require their inputs and outputs 
 to be discrete. Here we describe a generative autoregressive language model
  over the continuous space of protein backbones\, where the distribution ov
 er the placement of each successive amino acid is conditioned on all preced
 ing residues\, and can be sampled from one residue after another. We show t
 hat this approach can learn to sample diverse and realistic protein chains\
 , opening a new potential avenue for in silico protein design.<br><br><b>Me
 eting Details:<br></b><i>Every Monday at</i><i> </i><i>5pm CE(S)T / 11am ET
  / 8am PT.  </i><br><a href="https://zoom.us/j/5775722530?pwd=ZzlGTXlDNThhU
 DZOdU4vN2JRMm5pQT09" target="_blank">https://zoom.us/j/5775722530?pwd=ZzlGT
 XlDNThhUDZOdU4vN2JRMm5pQT09</a><br><br><b>Slack Workspace for discussion an
 d paper voting:<br></b><a href="https://join.slack.com/t/logag/shared_invit
 e/zt-22y7n3k7a-FHwX31gc85yZCa0uF8BU7w" target="_blank">https://join.slack.c
 om/t/logag/shared_invite/zt-22y7n3k7a-FHwX31gc85yZCa0uF8BU7w</a><br><br><b>
 All information: Schedule of upcoming papers\, recordings\, mailing list</b
 ><b>:<br></b><u><a href="https://hannes-stark.com/logag-reading-group" targ
 et="_blank">https://hannes-stark.com/logag-reading-group</a></u><b><br></b>
 <u></u><u></u><u></u><u></u><u></u>
LAST-MODIFIED:20240904T130202Z
LOCATION:https://zoom.us/j/5775722530?pwd=ZzlGTXlDNThhUDZOdU4vN2JRMm5pQT09
SEQUENCE:1
STATUS:CONFIRMED
SUMMARY:Learning on Graphs and Geometry Reading Group
