 ujn2pbpkhgr5f55cm39qbmu8@group.calendar.google.com
UID:4om8dkr7eu2gvvk4o4mi4c9bjg_R20231211T160000@google.com
ATTENDEE;CUTYPE=INDIVIDUAL;ROLE=REQ-PARTICIPANT;PARTSTAT=NEEDS-ACTION;CN=ma
 tzmon@nvidia.com;X-NUM-GUESTS=0:mailto:matzmon@nvidia.com
RECURRENCE-ID;TZID=Europe/Berlin:20240617T170000
CREATED:20210724T162031Z
DESCRIPTION:<u></u><u></u><u></u><u></u><u></u><b>Speaker:<br></b>Matan Atz
 mon from NVIDIA.<br><br><b>Paper:</b><br><b>Approximately Piecewise E(3) Eq
 uivariant Point Networks </b><a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2402.08529">htt
 ps://arxiv.org/abs/2402.08529</a> (<a href="https://arxiv.org/search/cs?sea
 rchtype=author&amp\;query=Atzmon\,+M"><u>Matan Atzmon</u></a>\, <a href="ht
 tps://arxiv.org/search/cs?searchtype=author&amp\;query=Huang\,+J">Jiahui Hu
 ang</a>\, <a href="https://arxiv.org/search/cs?searchtype=author&amp\;query
 =Williams\,+F">Francis Williams</a>\, <a href="https://arxiv.org/search/cs?
 searchtype=author&amp\;query=Litany\,+O">Or Litany</a>)<br>Integrating a no
 tion of symmetry into point cloud neural networks is a provably effective w
 ay to improve their generalization capability. Of particular interest are E
 (3) equivariant point cloud networks where Euclidean transformations applie
 d to the inputs are preserved in the outputs. Recent efforts aim to extend 
 networks that are E(3) equivariant\, to accommodate inputs made of multiple
  parts\, each of which exhibits local E(3) symmetry. In practical settings\
 , however\, the partitioning into individually transforming regions is unkn
 own a priori. Errors in the partition prediction would unavoidably map to e
 rrors in respecting the true input symmetry. Past works have proposed diffe
 rent ways to predict the partition\, which may exhibit uncontrolled errors 
 in their ability to maintain equivariance to the actual partition. To this 
 end\, we introduce APEN: a general framework for constructing approximate p
 iecewise-E(3) equivariant point networks. Our primary insight is that funct
 ions that are equivariant with respect to a finer partition will also maint
 ain equivariance in relation to the true partition. Leveraging this observa
 tion\, we propose a design where the equivariance approximation error at ea
 ch layers can be bounded solely in terms of (i) uncertainty quantification 
 of the partition prediction\, and (ii) bounds on the probability of failing
  to suggest a proper subpartition of the ground truth one. We demonstrate t
 he effectiveness of APEN using two data types exemplifying part-based symme
 try: (i) real-world scans of room scenes containing multiple furniture-type
  objects\; and\, (ii) human motions\, characterized by articulated parts ex
 hibiting rigid movement. Our empirical results demonstrate the advantage of
  integrating piecewise E(3) symmetry into network design\, showing a distin
 ct improvement in generalization compared to prior works for both classific
 ation and segmentation tasks.<br><b><br></b><b>Meeting Details:<br></b><i>E
 very Monday at</i><i> </i><i>5pm CST / 4pm UTC / 11am EST / 8am PST.  </i><
 br><a href="https://zoom.us/j/5775722530?pwd=ZzlGTXlDNThhUDZOdU4vN2JRMm5pQT
 09" target="_blank">https://zoom.us/j/5775722530?pwd=ZzlGTXlDNThhUDZOdU4vN2
 JRMm5pQT09</a><br><br><b>Slack Workspace for discussion and paper voting:<b
 r></b><a href="https://join.slack.com/t/logag/shared_invite/zt-22y7n3k7a-FH
 wX31gc85yZCa0uF8BU7w" target="_blank">https://join.slack.com/t/logag/shared
 _invite/zt-22y7n3k7a-FHwX31gc85yZCa0uF8BU7w</a><br><br><b>All information: 
 Schedule of upcoming papers\, recordings\, mailing list</b><b>:<br></b><u><
 a href="https://hannes-stark.com/logag-reading-group" target="_blank">https
 ://hannes-stark.com/logag-reading-group</a></u><b><br></b><u></u><u></u><u>
 </u><u></u><u></u>
LAST-MODIFIED:20240904T130202Z
