ORGANIZER;CN=vdujn2pbpkhgr5f55cm39qbmu8@group.calendar.google.com:mailto:vd
 ujn2pbpkhgr5f55cm39qbmu8@group.calendar.google.com
UID:FEEAEC38-0494-443E-BE84-F231E30E5AF5
ATTENDEE;CUTYPE=INDIVIDUAL;ROLE=REQ-PARTICIPANT;PARTSTAT=NEEDS-ACTION;CN=xi
 angfu@mit.edu;X-NUM-GUESTS=0:mailto:xiangfu@mit.edu
RECURRENCE-ID;TZID=Europe/Berlin:20220705T170000
CREATED:20220820T195616Z
DESCRIPTION:<html-blob><u></u><u></u><b>Speaker:<br></b>First author of the
  paper <a href="https://xiangfu.co/" id="ow904" __is_owner="true">Xiang Fu<
 /a>&nbsp\;who is a PhD student at MIT in the groups of&nbsp\;<a href="https
 ://people.csail.mit.edu/tommi/tommi.html">Tommi Jaakkola</a>&nbsp\;and&nbsp
 \;<a href="http://people.csail.mit.edu/pulkitag/">Pulkit Agrawal</a>.&nbsp\
 ;Currently\, he works on molecular dynamics and coarse-graining or other ab
 straction-related approaches.<br><br><b>Paper:</b><br><b>Simulate Time-inte
 grated Coarse-grained Molecular Dynamics with Geometric Machine Learning</b
 >&nbsp\;<a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2204.10348" id="ow923" __is_owner="t
 rue">https://arxiv.org/abs/2204.10348 </a>(<a href="https://arxiv.org/searc
 h/cs?searchtype=author&amp\;query=Fu%2C+X" class="paper-link" style="displa
 y: inline-block\;">Xiang Fu</a>\,&nbsp\;<a href="https://arxiv.org/search/c
 s?searchtype=author&amp\;query=Xie%2C+T" class="paper-link" style="display:
  inline-block\;">Tian Xie</a>\,&nbsp\;<a href="https://arxiv.org/search/cs?
 searchtype=author&amp\;query=Rebello%2C+N+J" class="paper-link" style="disp
 lay: inline-block\;">Nathan J. Rebello</a>\,&nbsp\;<a href="https://arxiv.o
 rg/search/cs?searchtype=author&amp\;query=Olsen%2C+B+D" class="paper-link" 
 style="display: inline-block\;">Bradley D. Olsen</a>\,&nbsp\;<a href="https
 ://arxiv.org/search/cs?searchtype=author&amp\;query=Jaakkola%2C+T" class="p
 aper-link" style="display: inline-block\;">Tommi Jaakkola</a>)<br>Molecular
  dynamics (MD) simulation is the workhorse of various scientific domains bu
 t is limited by high computational cost. Learning-based force fields have m
 ade major progress in accelerating ab-initio MD simulation but are still no
 t fast enough for many real-world applications that require long-time MD si
 mulation. In this paper\, we adopt a different machine learning approach wh
 ere we coarse-grain a physical system using graph clustering\, and model th
 e system evolution with a very large time-integration step using graph neur
 al networks. A novel score-based GNN refinement module resolves the long-st
 anding challenge of long-time simulation instability. Despite only trained 
 with short MD trajectory data\, our learned simulator can generalize to uns
 een novel systems and simulate for much longer than the training trajectori
 es. Properties requiring 10-100 ns level long-time dynamics can be accurate
 ly recovered at several-orders-of-magnitude higher speed than classical for
 ce fields. We demonstrate the effectiveness of our method on two realistic 
 complex systems: (1) single-chain coarse-grained polymers in implicit solve
 nt\; (2) multi-component Li-ion polymer electrolyte systems.<br><b><br></b>
 <b>Meeting Details:<br></b><i>Every Tuesday at</i><i> </i><i>5pm CEST / 3pm
  UTC / 11am EST / 8am PDT.&nbsp\;&nbsp\;</i><br><a href="https://zoom.us/j/
 5775722530?pwd=ZzlGTXlDNThhUDZOdU4vN2JRMm5pQT09">https://zoom.us/j/57757225
 30?pwd=ZzlGTXlDNThhUDZOdU4vN2JRMm5pQT09</a><br><br><b>Slack Workspace for d
 iscussion and paper voting:<br></b><a href="https://join.slack.com/t/logag/
 shared_invite/zt-u0mbo1ec-zElmvd1oSCXGjXvxLSokvg">https://join.slack.com/t/
 <u></u>logag/shared_invite/zt-<u></u>u0mbo1ec-<u></u>zElmvd1oSCXGjXvxLSokvg
 </a><br><br><b>All information: Schedule of upcoming papers\, recordings\, 
 mailing list</b><b>:<br></b><u><a href="https://hannes-stark.com/logag-read
 ing-group">https://hannes-stark.com/logag-reading-group</a></u><b><br></b><
 u></u><u></u></html-blob>
