DTSTART;TZID=Europe/Berlin:20260316T170000
DTEND;TZID=Europe/Berlin:20260316T180000
DTSTAMP:20260325T005103Z
ORGANIZER;CN=vdujn2pbpkhgr5f55cm39qbmu8@group.calendar.google.com:mailto:vd
 ujn2pbpkhgr5f55cm39qbmu8@group.calendar.google.com
UID:b1qril1ke5kft3h5gbg6tqm1ob_R20250630T160000@google.com
ATTENDEE;CUTYPE=INDIVIDUAL;ROLE=REQ-PARTICIPANT;PARTSTAT=ACCEPTED;CN=peterh
 olderrieth@gmail.com;X-NUM-GUESTS=0:mailto:peterholderrieth@gmail.com
RECURRENCE-ID;TZID=Europe/Berlin:20260316T180000
CREATED:20210724T162031Z
DESCRIPTION:<u></u><u></u><u></u><u></u><u></u><b>Speaker:<br></b>Peter Hol
 derrieth who is a PhD student at MIT working with Tommit Jaakkola.<br><br><
 b>Paper:</b><br><b>Diamond Maps: Efficient Reward Alignment via Stochastic 
 Flow Maps </b><a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.05993">https://arxiv.org/
 abs/2602.05993</a> (<a href="https://arxiv.org/search/cs?searchtype=author&
 amp\;query=Holderrieth\,+P">Peter Holderrieth</a>\, <a href="https://arxiv.
 org/search/cs?searchtype=author&amp\;query=Chen\,+D">Douglas Chen</a>\, <a 
 href="https://arxiv.org/search/cs?searchtype=author&amp\;query=Eyring\,+L">
 Luca Eyring</a>\, <a href="https://arxiv.org/search/cs?searchtype=author&am
 p\;query=Shah\,+I">Ishin Shah</a>\, <a href="https://arxiv.org/search/cs?se
 archtype=author&amp\;query=Anantharaman\,+G">Giri Anantharaman</a>\, <a hre
 f="https://arxiv.org/search/cs?searchtype=author&amp\;query=He\,+Y">Yutong 
 He</a>\, <a href="https://arxiv.org/search/cs?searchtype=author&amp\;query=
 Akata\,+Z">Zeynep Akata</a>\, <a href="https://arxiv.org/search/cs?searchty
 pe=author&amp\;query=Jaakkola\,+T">Tommi Jaakkola</a>\, <a href="https://ar
 xiv.org/search/cs?searchtype=author&amp\;query=Boffi\,+N+M">Nicholas Matthe
 w Boffi</a>\, <a href="https://arxiv.org/search/cs?searchtype=author&amp\;q
 uery=Simchowitz\,+M">Max Simchowitz</a>)<br>Flow and diffusion models produ
 ce high-quality samples\, but adapting them to user preferences or constrai
 nts post-training remains costly and brittle\, a challenge commonly called 
 reward alignment. We argue that efficient reward alignment should be a prop
 erty of the generative model itself\, not an afterthought\, and redesign th
 e model for adaptability. We propose "Diamond Maps"\, stochastic flow map m
 odels that enable efficient and accurate alignment to arbitrary rewards at 
 inference time. Diamond Maps amortize many simulation steps into a single-s
 tep sampler\, like flow maps\, while preserving the stochasticity required 
 for optimal reward alignment. This design makes search\, sequential Monte C
 arlo\, and guidance scalable by enabling efficient and consistent estimatio
 n of the value function. Our experiments show that Diamond Maps can be lear
 ned efficiently via distillation from GLASS Flows\, achieve stronger reward
  alignment performance\, and scale better than existing methods. Our result
 s point toward a practical route to generative models that can be rapidly a
 dapted to arbitrary preferences and constraints at inference time.<br><b><b
 r></b><b>Meeting Details:<br></b><i>Every Monday at 9</i><i>:00 PT / 12:00 
 ET /  18:00 CE(S)T  </i><br><a href="https://zoom.us/j/5775722530?pwd=ZzlGT
 XlDNThhUDZOdU4vN2JRMm5pQT09" target="_blank">https://zoom.us/j/5775722530?p
 wd=ZzlGTXlDNThhUDZOdU4vN2JRMm5pQT09</a><br><br><b>Slack Workspace for discu
 ssion and paper voting:<br></b><a href="https://join.slack.com/t/logag/shar
 ed_invite/zt-2zuxi7gd1-rLUgxg6gnCkhO7WlRsyElg" target="_blank"><u>https://j
 oin.slack.com/t/logag/shared_invite/zt-2zuxi7gd1-rLUgxg6gnCkhO7WlRsyElg</u>
 </a><br><br><b>All information: Schedule of upcoming papers\, recordings\, 
 mailing list</b><b>:<br></b><a href="https://portal.valencelabs.com/starkly
 speaking" target="_blank">https://portal.valencelabs.com/starklyspeaking</a
 ><b></b><u></u><u></u><u></u><u></u><u></u>
LAST-MODIFIED:20260320T170220Z
