ATTENDEE;CUTYPE=INDIVIDUAL;ROLE=REQ-PARTICIPANT;PARTSTAT=ACCEPTED;CN=cw.hua
 ng427@gmail.com;X-NUM-GUESTS=0:mailto:cw.huang427@gmail.com
ATTENDEE;CUTYPE=INDIVIDUAL;ROLE=REQ-PARTICIPANT;PARTSTAT=NEEDS-ACTION;CN=mi
 lad.aghajohari@umontreal.ca;X-NUM-GUESTS=0:mailto:milad.aghajohari@umontrea
 l.ca
RECURRENCE-ID;TZID=Europe/Berlin:20230508T170000
CREATED:20210724T162031Z
DESCRIPTION:<u></u><b>Speaker:<br></b>The authors <a href="https://chinweih
 uang.com/"><u>Chin-Wei Huang</u></a> and <a href="https://www.linkedin.com/
 in/milad-aghajohari-6b9a669b/?originalSubdomain=ir"><u>Milad Aghajohari</u>
 </a>. Chin-Wei is a senior researcher at MSR Amsterdam\, where he works on 
 molecular simulation using machine learning. He is also a final year PhD ca
 ndidate at Mila\, advised by Aaron Courville.  His PhD work is on probabili
 ty flows in deep learning\, including flow-based models\, latent variable m
 odels\, and diffusion models. Milad Aghajohari is a Masters student at Mila
 .<br><br><b>Paper:</b><br><b>Riemannian Diffusion Models </b><a href="https
 ://arxiv.org/abs/2208.07949"><u>https://arxiv.org/abs/2208.07949</u></a> (<
 a href="https://arxiv.org/search/cs?searchtype=author&query=Huang%2C+C"><u>
 <u>Chin-Wei Huang</u></u></a>\, <a href="https://arxiv.org/search/cs?search
 type=author&query=Aghajohari%2C+M"><u>Milad Aghajohari</u></a>\, <a href="h
 ttps://arxiv.org/search/cs?searchtype=author&query=Bose%2C+A+J"><u>Avishek 
 Joey Bose</u></a>\, <a href="https://arxiv.org/search/cs?searchtype=author&
 query=Panangaden%2C+P"><u>Prakash Panangaden</u></a>\, <a href="https://arx
 iv.org/search/cs?searchtype=author&query=Courville%2C+A"><u>Aaron Courville
 </u></a>)<br>Diffusion models are recent state-of-the-art methods for image
  generation and likelihood estimation. In this work\, we generalize continu
 ous-time diffusion models to arbitrary Riemannian manifolds and derive a va
 riational framework for likelihood estimation. Computationally\, we propose
  new methods for computing the Riemannian divergence which is needed in the
  likelihood estimation. Moreover\, in generalizing the Euclidean case\, we 
 prove that maximizing this variational lower-bound is equivalent to Riemann
 ian score matching. Empirically\, we demonstrate the expressive power of Ri
 emannian diffusion models on a wide spectrum of smooth manifolds\, such as 
 spheres\, tori\, hyperboloids\, and orthogonal groups. Our proposed method 
 achieves new state-of-the-art likelihoods on all benchmarks.<br><b><br></b>
 <b>Meeting Details:<br></b><i>Every Monday at</i><i> </i><i>5pm CEST / 3pm 
 UTC / 11am EDT / 8am PDT.  </i><br><a href="https://zoom.us/j/5775722530?pw
 d=ZzlGTXlDNThhUDZOdU4vN2JRMm5pQT09">https://zoom.us/j/5775722530?pwd=ZzlGTX
 lDNThhUDZOdU4vN2JRMm5pQT09</a><br><br><b>Slack Workspace for discussion and
  paper voting:<br></b><a href="https://join.slack.com/t/logag/shared_invite
 /zt-1hcqybjyd-ty5Pbj~BQy~W4B4mQQVxDQ">https://join.slack.com/t/logag/shared
 _invite/zt-1hcqybjyd-ty5Pbj~BQy~W4B4mQQVxDQ</a><br><br><b>All information: 
 Schedule of upcoming papers\, recordings\, mailing list</b><b>:<br></b><u><
 a href="https://hannes-stark.com/logag-reading-group">https://hannes-stark.
 com/logag-reading-group</a></u><b><br></b><u></u><u></u><u></u><u></u><u></
 u>
