BEGIN:VEVENT
DTSTART;TZID=Europe/Berlin:20230116T170000
DTEND;TZID=Europe/Berlin:20230116T180000
DTSTAMP:20260325T005103Z
ORGANIZER;CN=vdujn2pbpkhgr5f55cm39qbmu8@group.calendar.google.com:mailto:vd
 ujn2pbpkhgr5f55cm39qbmu8@group.calendar.google.com
UID:4om8dkr7eu2gvvk4o4mi4c9bjg_R20230102T160000@google.com
ATTENDEE;CUTYPE=INDIVIDUAL;ROLE=REQ-PARTICIPANT;PARTSTAT=ACCEPTED;CN=noamra
 zin@mail.tau.ac.il;X-NUM-GUESTS=0:mailto:noamrazin@mail.tau.ac.il
RECURRENCE-ID;TZID=Europe/Berlin:20230116T170000
CREATED:20210724T162031Z
DESCRIPTION:<u></u><u></u><u></u><u></u><u></u><b>Speaker:<br></b>Noam Razi
 n who is a PhD candidate at Tel Aviv University\, advised by&nbsp\;<a href=
 "https://www.cohennadav.com/">Nadav Cohen</a>.<br><br><b>Paper:</b><br><b>O
 n the Ability of Graph Neural Networks to Model Interactions Between Vertic
 es&nbsp\;</b><a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2211.16494" class="paper-link" 
 style="display: inline-block\;">https://arxiv.org/abs/2211.16494</a>&nbsp\;
 (<a href="https://arxiv.org/search/cs?searchtype=author&amp\;query=Razin%2C
 +N" class="paper-link" style="display: inline-block\;">Noam Razin</a>\,&nbs
 p\;<a href="https://arxiv.org/search/cs?searchtype=author&amp\;query=Verbin
 %2C+T" class="paper-link" style="display: inline-block\;">Tom Verbin</a>\,&
 nbsp\;<a href="https://arxiv.org/search/cs?searchtype=author&amp\;query=Coh
 en%2C+N" class="paper-link" style="display: inline-block\;">Nadav Cohen</a>
 )<br>Graph neural networks (GNNs) are widely used for modeling complex inte
 ractions between entities represented as vertices of a graph. Despite recen
 t efforts to theoretically analyze the expressive power of GNNs\, a formal 
 characterization of their ability to model interactions is lacking. The cur
 rent paper aims to address this gap. Formalizing strength of interactions t
 hrough an established measure known as separation rank\, we quantify the ab
 ility of certain GNNs to model interaction between a given subset of vertic
 es and its complement\, i.e. between sides of a given partition of input ve
 rtices. Our results reveal that the ability to model interaction is primari
 ly determined by the partition's walk index -- a graph-theoretical characte
 ristic that we define by the number of walks originating from the boundary 
 of the partition. Experiments with common GNN architectures corroborate thi
 s finding. As a practical application of our theory\, we design an edge spa
 rsification algorithm named Walk Index Sparsification (WIS)\, which preserv
 es the ability of a GNN to model interactions when input edges are removed.
  WIS is simple\, computationally efficient\, and markedly outperforms alter
 native methods in terms of induced prediction accuracy. More broadly\, it s
 howcases the potential of improving GNNs by theoretically analyzing the int
 eractions they can model.<br><br><b>Meeting Details:<br></b><i>Every Monday
  at</i><i>&nbsp\;</i><i>5pm CET / 4pm UTC / 11am EST / 8am PST.&nbsp\;&nbsp
 \;</i><br><a href="https://zoom.us/j/5775722530?pwd=ZzlGTXlDNThhUDZOdU4vN2J
 RMm5pQT09">https://zoom.us/j/5775722530?pwd=ZzlGTXlDNThhUDZOdU4vN2JRMm5pQT0
 9</a><br><br><b>Slack Workspace for discussion and paper voting:<br></b><a 
 href="https://join.slack.com/t/logag/shared_invite/zt-1hcqybjyd-ty5Pbj~BQy~
 W4B4mQQVxDQ">https://join.slack.com/t/logag/shared_invite/zt-1hcqybjyd-ty5P
 bj~BQy~W4B4mQQVxDQ</a><br><br><b>All information: Schedule of upcoming pape
 rs\, recordings\, mailing list</b><b>:<br></b><u><a href="https://hannes-st
 ark.com/logag-reading-group">https://hannes-stark.com/logag-reading-group</
 a></u><b><br></b><u></u><u></u><u></u><u></u><u></u>
LAST-MODIFIED:20240904T130202Z
