BEGIN:VEVENT
DTSTART;TZID=Europe/Berlin:20240415T170000
DTEND;TZID=Europe/Berlin:20240415T180000
DTSTAMP:20260325T005103Z
ORGANIZER;CN=vdujn2pbpkhgr5f55cm39qbmu8@group.calendar.google.com:mailto:vd
 ujn2pbpkhgr5f55cm39qbmu8@group.calendar.google.com
UID:4om8dkr7eu2gvvk4o4mi4c9bjg_R20231211T160000@google.com
ATTENDEE;CUTYPE=INDIVIDUAL;ROLE=REQ-PARTICIPANT;PARTSTAT=NEEDS-ACTION;CN=li
 or.yariv@weizmann.ac.il;X-NUM-GUESTS=0:mailto:lior.yariv@weizmann.ac.il
ATTENDEE;CUTYPE=INDIVIDUAL;ROLE=REQ-PARTICIPANT;PARTSTAT=NEEDS-ACTION;CN=he
 li.benhamu@weizmann.ac.il;X-NUM-GUESTS=0:mailto:heli.benhamu@weizmann.ac.il
RECURRENCE-ID;TZID=Europe/Berlin:20240415T170000
CREATED:20210724T162031Z
DESCRIPTION:<u></u><u></u><u></u><u></u><u></u><b>Speaker:<br></b>Lior Yari
 v is a PhD student in the Department of Computer Science and Applied Mathem
 atics at the Weizmann Institute of Science under the supervision of <a href
 ="http://www.wisdom.weizmann.ac.il/~ylipman/">Prof. Yaron Lipman</a>. Her m
 ain research interest is in devising deep learning methods for various 3D s
 hapes analysis and synthesis tasks.<br><br><b>Paper:</b><br><b>Mosaic-SDF f
 or 3D Generative Models </b><a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2312.09222">http
 s://arxiv.org/abs/2312.09222</a> (<a href="https://arxiv.org/search/cs?sear
 chtype=author&amp\;query=Yariv\,+L"><u>Lior Yariv</u></a>\, <a href="https:
 //arxiv.org/search/cs?searchtype=author&amp\;query=Puny\,+O">Omri Puny</a>\
 , <a href="https://arxiv.org/search/cs?searchtype=author&amp\;query=Neverov
 a\,+N">Natalia Neverova</a>\, <a href="https://arxiv.org/search/cs?searchty
 pe=author&amp\;query=Gafni\,+O">Oran Gafni</a>\, <a href="https://arxiv.org
 /search/cs?searchtype=author&amp\;query=Lipman\,+Y">Yaron Lipman</a>)<br>Cu
 rrent diffusion or flow-based generative models for 3D shapes divide to two
 : distilling pre-trained 2D image diffusion models\, and training directly 
 on 3D shapes. When training a diffusion or flow models on 3D shapes a cruci
 al design choice is the shape representation. An effective shape representa
 tion needs to adhere three design principles: it should allow an efficient 
 conversion of large 3D datasets to the representation form\; it should prov
 ide a good tradeoff of approximation power versus number of parameters\; an
 d it should have a simple tensorial form that is compatible with existing p
 owerful neural architectures. While standard 3D shape representations such 
 as volumetric grids and point clouds do not adhere to all these principles 
 simultaneously\, we advocate in this paper a new representation that does. 
 We introduce Mosaic-SDF (M-SDF): a simple 3D shape representation that appr
 oximates the Signed Distance Function (SDF) of a given shape by using a set
  of local grids spread near the shape's boundary. The M-SDF representation 
 is fast to compute for each shape individually making it readily paralleliz
 able\; it is parameter efficient as it only covers the space around the sha
 pe's boundary\; and it has a simple matrix form\, compatible with Transform
 er-based architectures. We demonstrate the efficacy of the M-SDF representa
 tion by using it to train a 3D generative flow model including class-condit
 ioned generation with the 3D Warehouse dataset\, and text-to-3D generation 
 using a dataset of about 600k caption-shape pairs.<br><b><br></b><b>Meeting
  Details:<br></b><i>Every Monday at</i><i> </i><i>5pm CST / 4pm UTC / 11am 
 EST / 8am PST.  </i><br><a href="https://zoom.us/j/5775722530?pwd=ZzlGTXlDN
