DTSTART;TZID=Europe/Berlin:20210914T170000
DTEND;TZID=Europe/Berlin:20210914T180000
DTSTAMP:20260325T005103Z
ORGANIZER;CN=vdujn2pbpkhgr5f55cm39qbmu8@group.calendar.google.com:mailto:vd
 ujn2pbpkhgr5f55cm39qbmu8@group.calendar.google.com
UID:1hfd2s2r4hmta9og6idelbrfic_R20210824T150000@google.com
ATTENDEE;CUTYPE=INDIVIDUAL;ROLE=REQ-PARTICIPANT;PARTSTAT=ACCEPTED;CN=gbouri
 tsas@gmail.com;X-NUM-GUESTS=0:mailto:gbouritsas@gmail.com
ATTENDEE;CUTYPE=INDIVIDUAL;ROLE=REQ-PARTICIPANT;PARTSTAT=ACCEPTED;CN=loukas
 an@gmail.com;X-NUM-GUESTS=0:mailto:loukasan@gmail.com
RECURRENCE-ID;TZID=Europe/Berlin:20210914T170000
CREATED:20210724T162031Z
DESCRIPTION:<b>Speaker:<br></b>The main presenter will be first author&nbsp
 \;<a href="https://www.linkedin.com/in/giorgos-bouritsas/?originalSubdomain
 =uk">Giorgos Bouritsas</a>&nbsp\;who is a PhD student at Imperial College L
 ondon under the supervision of Prof. Michael Bronstein and he is currently 
 a visiting PhD at <a href="http://epfl.ch">EPFL</a>\, Switzerland.&nbsp\;Al
 so joining us will be paper author Dr.&nbsp\;<a href="https://andreasloukas
 .blog/" id="ow1738" __is_owner="true">Andreas Loukas</a>&nbsp\;who is&nbsp\
 ;a research scientist (Ambizione fellow) at the&nbsp\;<a href="https://lts2
 .epfl.ch/">LTS2</a>&nbsp\;lab in EPFL.<br><br><b>Paper:</b><br><b>Partition
  and Code: learning how to compress graphs</b>&nbsp\;<a href="https://arxiv
 .org/abs/2107.01952">https://arxiv.org/abs/2107.01952</a>&nbsp\;(<a href="h
 ttps://arxiv.org/search/cs?searchtype=author&query=Bouritsas%2C+G">Giorgos 
 Bouritsas</a>\,&nbsp\;<a href="https://arxiv.org/search/cs?searchtype=autho
 r&query=Loukas%2C+A">Andreas Loukas</a>\,&nbsp\;<a href="https://arxiv.org/
 search/cs?searchtype=author&query=Karalias%2C+N">Nikolaos Karalias</a>\,&nb
 sp\;<a href="https://arxiv.org/search/cs?searchtype=author&query=Bronstein%
 2C+M+M">Michael M. Bronstein</a>)<br>Can we use machine learning to compres
 s graph data? The absence of ordering in graphs poses a significant challen
 ge to conventional compression algorithms\, limiting their attainable gains
  as well as their ability to discover relevant patterns. On the other hand\
 , most graph compression approaches rely on domain-dependent handcrafted re
 presentations and cannot adapt to different underlying graph distributions.
  This work aims to establish the necessary principles a lossless graph comp
 ression method should follow to approach the entropy storage lower bound. I
 nstead of making rigid assumptions about the graph distribution\, we formul
 ate the compressor as a probabilistic model that can be learned from data a
 nd generalise to unseen instances. Our "Partition and Code" framework entai
 ls three steps: first\, a partitioning algorithm decomposes the graph into 
 elementary structures\, then these are mapped to the elements of a small di
 ctionary on which we learn a probability distribution\, and finally\, an en
 tropy encoder translates the representation into bits. All three steps are 
 parametric and can be trained with gradient descent. We theoretically compa
 re the compression quality of several graph encodings and prove\, under mil
 d conditions\, a total ordering of their expected description lengths. More
 over\, we show that\, under the same conditions\, PnC achieves compression 
 gains w.r.t. the baselines that grow either linearly or quadratically with 
 the number of vertices. Our algorithms are quantitatively evaluated on dive
 rse real-world networks obtaining significant performance improvements with
  respect to different families of non-parametric and parametric graph compr
 essors.<br><b><br></b><b>Meeting Details:<br></b><i>Every Tuesday at 5pm CE
 ST / 3pm UTC / 11am EST / 8am PST</i><br><a href="https://us02web.zoom.us/j
 /4556095405?pwd=UmZRbC95d050TkRsZ2QvWER3SDdqUT09">https://us02web.zoom.us/j
 /4556095405?pwd=UmZRbC95d050TkRsZ2QvWER3SDdqUT09</a><br>Meeting ID: 455 609
  5405<br>Passcode: logag<br><br><b>Join Slack Workspace for discussions bey
 ond the meetings and paper voting:<br></b><a href="https://join.slack.com/t
 /logag/shared_invite/zt-u0mbo1ec-zElmvd1oSCXGjXvxLSokvg">https://join.slack
 .com/t/<u></u>logag/shared_invite/zt-<u></u>u0mbo1ec-<u></u>zElmvd1oSCXGjXv
 xLSokvg</a><br><br><b>All information: Schedule of upcoming talks\, slides 
 + notes of previously covered papers</b><b>:<br></b><a href="http:///">http
 s://hannes-stark.com/logag-reading-group</a><b><br></b>
LAST-MODIFIED:20220820T195612Z
SEQUENCE:0
STATUS:CONFIRMED
SUMMARY:Learning on Graphs and Geometry Reading Group
TRANSP:OPAQUE
