ORGANIZER;CN=vdujn2pbpkhgr5f55cm39qbmu8@group.calendar.google.com:mailto:vd
 ujn2pbpkhgr5f55cm39qbmu8@group.calendar.google.com
UID:1hfd2s2r4hmta9og6idelbrfic_R20220118T160000@google.com
ATTENDEE;CUTYPE=INDIVIDUAL;ROLE=REQ-PARTICIPANT;PARTSTAT=ACCEPTED;CN=johann
 es.lutzeyer@googlemail.com;X-NUM-GUESTS=0:mailto:johannes.lutzeyer@googlema
 il.com
ATTENDEE;CUTYPE=INDIVIDUAL;ROLE=REQ-PARTICIPANT;PARTSTAT=NEEDS-ACTION;CN=ge
 orge.dasoulas1@gmail.com;X-NUM-GUESTS=0:mailto:george.dasoulas1@gmail.com
RECURRENCE-ID;TZID=Europe/Berlin:20220125T170000
CREATED:20210724T162031Z
DESCRIPTION:<html-blob><b>Speakers:<br></b><a href="https://gdasoulas.githu
 b.io/">George Dasoulas</a>\,&nbsp\;who is a final year PhD student in both 
 the&nbsp\;<a href="https://www.lix.polytechnique.fr/dascim/">Data Science a
 nd Mining group</a>&nbsp\;at the Ecole Polytechnique and&nbsp\;<a href="htt
 ps://www.noahlab.com.hk/#/home">Noah’s Ark Lab</a>&nbsp\;of Huawei Technolo
 gies France. Under the supervision of&nbsp\;<a href="https://www.lix.polyte
 chnique.fr/Labo/Michalis.Vazirgiannis/">Prof. Michalis Vazirgiannis</a>\,&n
 bsp\;he works in the field of Graph Representation Learning\, studying the 
 expressive power of Graph Neural Networks (GNNs). Using the gained theoreti
 cal understanding of GNN architectures\, his goal is to make impactful cont
 ributions to real-world problems that are characterized by an inherent grap
 h structure ranging from communication graphs to molecular networks.&nbsp\;
 <a href="https://scholar.google.com/citations?user=OfT4ns8AAAAJ&hl=en&oi=ao
 ">Dr. Johannes Lutzeyer</a>&nbsp\;is currently a postdoctoral researcher in
  the&nbsp\;<a href="https://www.lix.polytechnique.fr/dascim/">Data Science 
 and Mining group</a>&nbsp\;at the Ecole Polytechnique. He works in the fiel
 d of Graph Representation Learning with a focus on Graph Neural Networks an
 d their theoretical analysis. He completed his&nbsp\;<a href="https://t.co/
 RAnht6Nh9R">PhD thesis</a>&nbsp\;at Imperial College London\, where he stud
 ied the spectral properties of different graph representation matrices such
  as the adjacency and Laplacian matrices.<br><br><b>Paper:</b><br><b>Learni
 ng Parametrised Graph Shift Operators&nbsp\;</b><a href="https://arxiv.org/
 abs/2101.10050" class="paper-link" style="display: inline-block\;">https://
 arxiv.org/abs/2101.10050&nbsp\;</a>(<a href="https://arxiv.org/search/?quer
 y=george+dasoulas&searchtype=all&abstracts=show&order=-announced_date_first
 &size=50">George Dasoulas</a>\,&nbsp\;<a href="https://arxiv.org/search/?qu
 ery=johannes+lutzeyer&searchtype=all&source=header">Johannes Lutzeyer</a>\,
 &nbsp\;<a href="https://arxiv.org/search/?query=michalis+vazirgiannis&searc
 htype=all&abstracts=show&order=-announced_date_first&size=50">Michalis Vazi
 rgiannis</a>)<br>In many domains data is currently represented as graphs an
 d therefore\, the graph representation of this data becomes increasingly im
 portant in machine learning. Network data is\, implicitly or explicitly\, a
 lways represented using a graph shift operator (GSO) with the most common c
 hoices being the adjacency\, Laplacian matrices and their normalisations. I
 n this paper\, a novel parametrised GSO (PGSO) is proposed\, where specific
  parameter values result in the most commonly used GSOs and message-passing
  operators in graph neural network (GNN) frameworks. The PGSO is suggested 
 as a replacement of the standard GSOs that are used in state-of-the-art GNN
  architectures and the optimisation of the PGSO parameters is seamlessly in
 cluded in the model training. It is proved that the PGSO has real eigenvalu
 es and a set of real eigenvectors independent of the parameter values and s
 pectral bounds on the PGSO are derived. PGSO parameters are shown to adapt 
 to the sparsity of the graph structure in a study on stochastic blockmodel 
 networks\, where they are found to automatically replicate the GSO regulari
 sation found in the literature. On several real-world datasets the accuracy
  of state-of-the-art GNN architectures is improved by the inclusion of the 
 PGSO in both node- and graph-classification tasks.<br><b><br></b><b>Meeting
  Details:<br></b><i>Every Tuesday at 5pm CET / 4pm UTC / 11am EDT / 8am PT<
 /i><br><a href="https://zoom.us/j/5775722530?pwd=ZzlGTXlDNThhUDZOdU4vN2JRMm
 5pQT09">https://zoom.us/j/5775722530?pwd=ZzlGTXlDNThhUDZOdU4vN2JRMm5pQT09</
 a><br><br><b>Slack Workspace for discussion and paper voting:<br></b><a hre
 f="https://join.slack.com/t/logag/shared_invite/zt-u0mbo1ec-zElmvd1oSCXGjXv
 xLSokvg">https://join.slack.com/t/<u></u>logag/shared_invite/zt-<u></u>u0mb
 o1ec-<u></u>zElmvd1oSCXGjXvxLSokvg</a><br><br><b>All information: Schedule 
 of upcoming papers\, recordings\, mailing list</b><b>:<br></b><u><a href="h
 ttps://hannes-stark.com/logag-reading-group">https://hannes-stark.com/logag
 -reading-group</a></u><b><br></b></html-blob>
LAST-MODIFIED:20220820T195612Z
