DTSTAMP:20260325T005103Z
ORGANIZER;CN=vdujn2pbpkhgr5f55cm39qbmu8@group.calendar.google.com:mailto:vd
 ujn2pbpkhgr5f55cm39qbmu8@group.calendar.google.com
UID:FEEAEC38-0494-443E-BE84-F231E30E5AF5
ATTENDEE;CUTYPE=INDIVIDUAL;ROLE=REQ-PARTICIPANT;PARTSTAT=ACCEPTED;CN=bugkjw
 @gmail.com;X-NUM-GUESTS=0:mailto:bugkjw@gmail.com
ATTENDEE;CUTYPE=INDIVIDUAL;ROLE=REQ-PARTICIPANT;PARTSTAT=NEEDS-ACTION;CN=ji
 nwoo-kim@kaist.ac.kr;X-NUM-GUESTS=0:mailto:jinwoo-kim@kaist.ac.kr
RECURRENCE-ID;TZID=Europe/Berlin:20220809T170000
CREATED:20220820T195614Z
DESCRIPTION:<html-blob><u></u><u></u><u></u><b>Speaker:<br></b>First author
  of the paper<a href="https://jw9730.notion.site/jw9730/Jinwoo-Kim-05607954
 27964cafa7481dc448baa4aa"> Jinwoo Kim</a> who is a Ph.D. student at <a href
 ="https://vllab.kaist.ac.kr/">Vision and Learning Laboratory</a> at <a href
 ="https://www.kaist.ac.kr/en/">KAIST</a>\, advised by <a href="https://maga
 33.github.io/">Prof. Seunghoon Hong</a>.<br><br><b>Paper:</b><br><b>Pure Tr
 ansformers are Powerful Graph Learners&nbsp\;</b><a href="https://arxiv.org
 /abs/2207.02505" class="paper-link" style="display: inline-block\;">https:/
 /arxiv.org/abs/2207.02505</a>&nbsp\;<b>(</b><a href="https://arxiv.org/sear
 ch/cs?searchtype=author&query=Kim%2C+J">Jinwoo Kim</a>\,&nbsp\;<a href="htt
 ps://arxiv.org/search/cs?searchtype=author&query=Nguyen%2C+T+D">Tien Dat Ng
 uyen</a>\,&nbsp\;<a href="https://arxiv.org/search/cs?searchtype=author&que
 ry=Min%2C+S">Seonwoo Min</a>\,&nbsp\;<a href="https://arxiv.org/search/cs?s
 earchtype=author&query=Cho%2C+S">Sungjun Cho</a>\,&nbsp\;<a href="https://a
 rxiv.org/search/cs?searchtype=author&query=Lee%2C+M">Moontae Lee</a>\,&nbsp
 \;<a href="https://arxiv.org/search/cs?searchtype=author&query=Lee%2C+H">Ho
 nglak Lee</a>\,&nbsp\;<a href="https://arxiv.org/search/cs?searchtype=autho
 r&query=Hong%2C+S">Seunghoon Hong</a>)<br>We show that standard Transformer
 s without graph-specific modifications can lead to promising results in gra
 ph learning both in theory and practice. Given a graph\, we simply treat al
 l nodes and edges as independent tokens\, augment them with token embedding
 s\, and feed them to a Transformer. With an appropriate choice of token emb
 eddings\, we prove that this approach is theoretically at least as expressi
 ve as an invariant graph network (2-IGN) composed of equivariant linear lay
 ers\, which is already more expressive than all message-passing Graph Neura
 l Networks (GNN). When trained on a large-scale graph dataset (PCQM4Mv2)\, 
 our method coined Tokenized Graph Transformer (TokenGT) achieves significan
 tly better results compared to GNN baselines and competitive results compar
 ed to Transformer variants with sophisticated graph-specific inductive bias
 . Our implementation is available at&nbsp\;<a href="https://github.com/jw97
 30/tokengt">this https URL</a>.<br><b><br></b><b>Meeting Details:<br></b><i
 >Every Tuesday at</i><i> </i><i>5pm CEST / 3pm UTC / 11am EST / 8am PDT.&nb
 sp\;&nbsp\;</i><br><a href="https://zoom.us/j/5775722530?pwd=ZzlGTXlDNThhUD
 ZOdU4vN2JRMm5pQT09">https://zoom.us/j/5775722530?pwd=ZzlGTXlDNThhUDZOdU4vN2
 JRMm5pQT09</a><br><br><b>Slack Workspace for discussion and paper voting:<b
 r></b><a href="https://join.slack.com/t/logag/shared_invite/zt-u0mbo1ec-zEl
 mvd1oSCXGjXvxLSokvg">https://join.slack.com/t/<u></u>logag/shared_invite/zt
 -<u></u>u0mbo1ec-<u></u>zElmvd1oSCXGjXvxLSokvg</a><br><br><b>All informatio
 n: Schedule of upcoming papers\, recordings\, mailing list</b><b>:<br></b><
 u><a href="https://hannes-stark.com/logag-reading-group">https://hannes-sta
 rk.com/logag-reading-group</a></u><b><br></b><u></u><u></u><u></u></html-bl
 ob>
LAST-MODIFIED:20220820T195616Z
