DTSTART;TZID=Europe/Berlin:20220301T170000
DTEND;TZID=Europe/Berlin:20220301T180000
DTSTAMP:20260325T005103Z
ORGANIZER;CN=vdujn2pbpkhgr5f55cm39qbmu8@group.calendar.google.com:mailto:vd
 ujn2pbpkhgr5f55cm39qbmu8@group.calendar.google.com
UID:1hfd2s2r4hmta9og6idelbrfic_R20220118T160000@google.com
ATTENDEE;CUTYPE=INDIVIDUAL;ROLE=REQ-PARTICIPANT;PARTSTAT=ACCEPTED;CN=emanue
 le.rossi1909@gmail.com;X-NUM-GUESTS=0:mailto:emanuele.rossi1909@gmail.com
X-GOOGLE-CONFERENCE:https://meet.google.com/vdz-nmos-tnw
RECURRENCE-ID;TZID=Europe/Berlin:20220301T170000
CREATED:20210724T162031Z
DESCRIPTION:<html-blob><b>Speaker:<br></b>First author <a href="https://www
 .emanuelerossi.co.uk/" id="ow607" __is_owner="true">Emanuele Rossi</a> who 
 is a Machine Learning Researcher at Twitter and a Ph.D. student at Imperial
  College London\, working on Graph Neural Networks and supervised by Prof. 
 Michael Bronstein. His research interests span a wide array of topics aroun
 d graph neural networks\, including scalability\, dynamic graphs\, and lear
 ning with missing node features. Before his current position\, Emanuele was
  working at Fabula AI\, which was then acquired by Twitter in June 2019. Pr
 eviously\, he completed an MPhil at the University of Cambridge and a BEng 
 at Imperial College London\, both in Computer Science.</html-blob><br><html
 -blob><br><b>Paper:</b><br><b>On the Unreasonable Effectiveness of Feature 
 propagation in Learning on Graphs with Missing Node Features&nbsp\;</b><a h
 ref="https://arxiv.org/abs/2111.12128">https://arxiv.org/abs/2111.12128</a>
 <b>&nbsp\;(</b><a href="https://arxiv.org/search/cs?searchtype=author&amp\;
 query=Rossi%2C+E">Emanuele Rossi</a>\,&nbsp\;<a href="https://arxiv.org/sea
 rch/cs?searchtype=author&amp\;query=Kenlay%2C+H">Henry Kenlay</a>\,&nbsp\;<
 a href="https://arxiv.org/search/cs?searchtype=author&amp\;query=Gorinova%2
 C+M+I">Maria I. Gorinova</a>\,&nbsp\;<a href="https://arxiv.org/search/cs?s
 earchtype=author&amp\;query=Chamberlain%2C+B+P">Benjamin Paul Chamberlain</
 a>\,&nbsp\;<a href="https://arxiv.org/search/cs?searchtype=author&amp\;quer
 y=Dong%2C+X">Xiaowen Dong</a>\,&nbsp\;<a href="https://arxiv.org/search/cs?
 searchtype=author&amp\;query=Bronstein%2C+M">Michael Bronstein</a>) 2021<br
 >While Graph Neural Networks (GNNs) have recently become the de facto stand
 ard for modeling relational data\, they impose a strong assumption on the a
 vailability of the node or edge features of the graph. In many real-world a
 pplications\, however\, features are only partially available\; for example
 \, in social networks\, age and gender are available only for a small subse
 t of users. We present a general approach for handling missing features in 
 graph machine learning applications that is based on minimization of the Di
 richlet energy and leads to a diffusion-type differential equation on the g
 raph. The discretization of this equation produces a simple\, fast and scal
 able algorithm which we call Feature Propagation. We experimentally show th
 at the proposed approach outperforms previous methods on seven common node-
 classification benchmarks and can withstand surprisingly high rates of miss
 ing features: on average we observe only around 4% relative accuracy drop w
 hen 99% of the features are missing. Moreover\, it takes only 10 seconds to
  run on a graph with&nbsp\;∼2.5M nodes and&nbsp\;∼123M edges on a single GP
 U.<br><b><br></b><b>Meeting Details:<br></b><i>Every Tuesday at 5pm CET / 4
 pm UTC / 11am EDT / 8am PT</i><br><a href="https://zoom.us/j/5775722530?pwd
 =ZzlGTXlDNThhUDZOdU4vN2JRMm5pQT09">https://zoom.us/j/5775722530?pwd=ZzlGTXl
 DNThhUDZOdU4vN2JRMm5pQT09</a><br><br><b>Slack Workspace for discussion and 
 paper voting:<br></b><a href="https://join.slack.com/t/logag/shared_invite/
 zt-u0mbo1ec-zElmvd1oSCXGjXvxLSokvg">https://join.slack.com/t/<u></u>logag/s
 hared_invite/zt-<u></u>u0mbo1ec-<u></u>zElmvd1oSCXGjXvxLSokvg</a><br><br><b
 >All information: Schedule of upcoming papers\, recordings\, mailing list</
 b><b>:<br></b><u><a href="https://hannes-stark.com/logag-reading-group">htt
 ps://hannes-stark.com/logag-reading-group</a></u><b><br></b></html-blob>\n\
 n-::~:~::~:~:~:~:~:~:~:~:~:~:~:~:~:~:~:~:~:~:~:~:~:~:~:~:~:~:~:~:~:~:~:~:~:
 ~:~:~::~:~::-\nJoin with Google Meet: https://meet.google.com/vdz-nmos-tnw\
 n\nLearn more about Meet at: https://support.google.com/a/users/answer/9282
 720\n\nPlease do not edit this section.\n-::~:~::~:~:~:~:~:~:~:~:~:~:~:~:~:
 ~:~:~:~:~:~:~:~:~:~:~:~:~:~:~:~:~:~:~:~:~:~:~::~:~::-
