BEGIN:VEVENT
DTSTART;TZID=Europe/Berlin:20211102T170000
DTEND;TZID=Europe/Berlin:20211102T180000
DTSTAMP:20260325T005103Z
ORGANIZER;CN=vdujn2pbpkhgr5f55cm39qbmu8@group.calendar.google.com:mailto:vd
 ujn2pbpkhgr5f55cm39qbmu8@group.calendar.google.com
UID:1hfd2s2r4hmta9og6idelbrfic_R20210824T150000@google.com
ATTENDEE;CUTYPE=INDIVIDUAL;ROLE=REQ-PARTICIPANT;PARTSTAT=ACCEPTED;CN=tianlo
 ng.chen@utexas.edu;X-NUM-GUESTS=0:mailto:tianlong.chen@utexas.edu
RECURRENCE-ID;TZID=Europe/Berlin:20211102T170000
CREATED:20210724T162031Z
DESCRIPTION:<b>Speaker:<br></b>First author&nbsp\;<a href="https://tianlong
 -chen.github.io/about/">Tianlong Chen (陈天龙)</a> who is a third-year Ph.D. s
 tudent of Electrical and Computer Engineering (<a href="http://www.ece.utex
 as.edu/research/areas/commnets">DICE</a>) at&nbsp\;<a href="https://vita-gr
 oup.github.io/">VITA</a>\, The University of Texas at Austin\, advised by&n
 bsp\;<a href="https://vita-group.github.io/">Dr. Zhangyang (Atlas) Wang</a>
 . His research interests include AutoML\, Adversarial Robustness\, Self-Sup
 ervision\, and Graph Neural Networks.<br><br><b>Paper:</b><br><b>A Unified 
 Lottery Ticket Hypothesis for Graph Neural Networks&nbsp\;</b><a href="http
 s://arxiv.org/abs/2102.06790">https://arxiv.org/abs/2102.06790</a>&nbsp\;(<
 a href="https://arxiv.org/search/cs?searchtype=author&query=Chen%2C+T">Tian
 long Chen</a>\,&nbsp\;<a href="https://arxiv.org/search/cs?searchtype=autho
 r&query=Sui%2C+Y">Yongduo Sui</a>\,&nbsp\;<a href="https://arxiv.org/search
 /cs?searchtype=author&query=Chen%2C+X">Xuxi Chen</a>\,&nbsp\;<a href="https
 ://arxiv.org/search/cs?searchtype=author&query=Zhang%2C+A">Aston Zhang</a>\
 ,&nbsp\;<a href="https://arxiv.org/search/cs?searchtype=author&query=Wang%2
 C+Z">Zhangyang Wang</a>)<br>With graphs rapidly growing in size and deeper 
 graph neural networks (GNNs) emerging\, the training and inference of GNNs 
 become increasingly expensive. Existing network weight pruning algorithms c
 annot address the main space and computational bottleneck in GNNs\, caused 
 by the size and connectivity of the graph. To this end\, this paper first p
 resents a unified GNN sparsification (UGS) framework that simultaneously pr
 unes the graph adjacency matrix and the model weights\, for effectively acc
 elerating GNN inference on large-scale graphs. Leveraging this new tool\, w
 e further generalize the recently popular lottery ticket hypothesis to GNNs
  for the first time\, by defining a graph lottery ticket (GLT) as a pair of
  core sub-dataset and sparse sub-network\, which can be jointly identified 
 from the original GNN and the full dense graph by iteratively applying UGS.
  Like its counterpart in convolutional neural networks\, GLT can be trained
  in isolation to match the performance of training with the full model and 
 graph\, and can be drawn from both randomly initialized and self-supervised
  pre-trained GNNs. Our proposal has been experimentally verified across var
 ious GNN architectures and diverse tasks\, on both small-scale graph datase
 ts (Cora\, Citeseer and PubMed)\, and large-scale datasets from the challen
 ging Open Graph Benchmark (OGB). Specifically\, for node classification\, o
 ur found GLTs achieve the same accuracies with 20%~98% MACs saving on small
  graphs and 25%~85% MACs saving on large ones. For link prediction\, GLTs l
 ead to 48%~97% and 70% MACs saving on small and large graph datasets\, resp
 ectively\, without compromising predictive performance. Codes available at&
 nbsp\;<a href="https://github.com/VITA-Group/Unified-LTH-GNN">this https UR
 L</a>.<br><b><br></b><b>Meeting Details:<br></b><i>Every Tuesday at 5pm CET
  / 4pm UTC / 12am EDT / 9am PT</i><br><a href="https://us02web.zoom.us/j/45
 56095405?pwd=UmZRbC95d050TkRsZ2QvWER3SDdqUT09">https://us02web.zoom.us/j/45
 56095405?pwd=UmZRbC95d050TkRsZ2QvWER3SDdqUT09</a><br><br><b>Join Slack Work
 space for discussions beyond the meetings and paper voting:<br></b><a href=
 "https://join.slack.com/t/logag/shared_invite/zt-u0mbo1ec-zElmvd1oSCXGjXvxL
 Sokvg">https://join.slack.com/t/<u></u>logag/shared_invite/zt-<u></u>u0mbo1
 ec-<u></u>zElmvd1oSCXGjXvxLSokvg</a><br><br><b>All information: Schedule of
  upcoming talks\, slides + notes of previously covered papers</b><b>:<br></
 b><a href="https://hannes-stark.com/logag-reading-group" id="ow1042" __is_o
 wner="true">https://hannes-stark.com/logag-reading-group</a><b><br></b>
LAST-MODIFIED:20220820T195612Z
SEQUENCE:0
STATUS:CONFIRMED
SUMMARY:Learning on Graphs and Geometry Reading Group
TRANSP:OPAQUE
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
DTSTART;TZID=Europe/Berlin:20220412T170000
DTEND;TZID=Europe/Berlin:20220412T180000
DTSTAMP:20260325T005103Z
ORGANIZER;CN=vdujn2pbpkhgr5f55cm39qbmu8@group.calendar.google.com:mailto:vd
 ujn2pbpkhgr5f55cm39qbmu8@group.calendar.google.com
UID:1hfd2s2r4hmta9og6idelbrfic_R20220405T150000@google.com
ATTENDEE;CUTYPE=INDIVIDUAL;ROLE=REQ-PARTICIPANT;PARTSTAT=ACCEPTED;CN=derekl
 im@mit.edu;X-NUM-GUESTS=0:mailto:dereklim@mit.edu
ATTENDEE;CUTYPE=INDIVIDUAL;ROLE=REQ-PARTICIPANT;PARTSTAT=NEEDS-ACTION;CN=jo
 shrob@mit.edu;X-NUM-GUESTS=0:mailto:joshrob@mit.edu
RECURRENCE-ID;TZID=Europe/Berlin:20220412T170000
CREATED:20210724T162031Z
DESCRIPTION:<html-blob><u></u><u></u><u></u><b>Speaker:<br></b>First author
 s <a href="https://cptq.github.io/">Derek Lim</a> and <a href="https://josh
 robinson.mit.edu/">Joshua Robinson</a>\, who both are PhD students in the g
 roup of Prof. Stefanie Jegelka at MIT. Josh is also co-advised by Prof. Suv
 rit Sra and Derek previously presented <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2110.
 02910">ESAN</a> in the reading group!<br><br><b>Paper:</b><br><b>Sign and B
 asis Invariant Networks for Spectral Graph Representation Learning&nbsp\;</
 b><a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2202.13013">https://arxiv.org/abs/2202.130
 13</a><b>&nbsp\;(</b><a href="https://arxiv.org/search/cs?searchtype=author
 &query=Lim%2C+D">Derek Lim</a>\,&nbsp\;<a href="https://arxiv.org/search/cs
 ?searchtype=author&query=Robinson%2C+J">Joshua Robinson</a>\,&nbsp\;<a href
 ="https://arxiv.org/search/cs?searchtype=author&query=Zhao%2C+L">Lingxiao Z
 hao</a>\,&nbsp\;<a href="https://arxiv.org/search/cs?searchtype=author&quer
 y=Smidt%2C+T">Tess Smidt</a>\,&nbsp\;<a href="https://arxiv.org/search/cs?s
 earchtype=author&query=Sra%2C+S">Suvrit Sra</a>\,&nbsp\;<a href="https://ar
 xiv.org/search/cs?searchtype=author&query=Maron%2C+H">Haggai Maron</a>\,&nb
 sp\;<a href="https://arxiv.org/search/cs?searchtype=author&query=Jegelka%2C
 +S">Stefanie Jegelka</a>) 2022<br>Many machine learning tasks involve proce
 ssing eigenvectors derived from data. Especially valuable are Laplacian eig
 envectors\, which capture useful structural information about graphs and ot
 her geometric objects. However\, ambiguities arise when computing eigenvect
 ors: for each eigenvector&nbsp\;v\, the sign flipped&nbsp\;−v&nbsp\;is also
  an eigenvector. More generally\, higher dimensional eigenspaces contain in
 finitely many choices of basis eigenvectors. These ambiguities make it a ch
 allenge to process eigenvectors and eigenspaces in a consistent way. In thi
 s work we introduce SignNet and BasisNet -- new neural architectures that a
 re invariant to all requisite symmetries and hence process collections of e
 igenspaces in a principled manner. Our networks are universal\, i.e.\, they
  can approximate any continuous function of eigenvectors with the proper in
 variances. They are also theoretically strong for graph representation lear
 ning -- they can approximate any spectral graph convolution\, can compute s
 pectral invariants that go beyond message passing neural networks\, and can
  provably simulate previously proposed graph positional encodings. Experime
 nts show the strength of our networks for learning spectral graph filters a
 nd learning graph positional encodings.<br><b><br></b><b>Meeting Details:<b
 r></b><i>Every Tuesday at</i><i> </i><i>5pm CEST / 3pm UTC / 11am EST / 8am
  PDT.&nbsp\;&nbsp\;</i><br><a href="https://zoom.us/j/5775722530?pwd=ZzlGTX
 lDNThhUDZOdU4vN2JRMm5pQT09">https://zoom.us/j/5775722530?pwd=ZzlGTXlDNThhUD
 ZOdU4vN2JRMm5pQT09</a><br><br><b>Slack Workspace for discussion and paper v
 oting:<br></b><a href="https://join.slack.com/t/logag/shared_invite/zt-u0mb
 o1ec-zElmvd1oSCXGjXvxLSokvg">https://join.slack.com/t/<u></u>logag/shared_i
 nvite/zt-<u></u>u0mbo1ec-<u></u>zElmvd1oSCXGjXvxLSokvg</a><br><br><b>All in
 formation: Schedule of upcoming papers\, recordings\, mailing list</b><b>:<
 br></b><u><a href="https://hannes-stark.com/logag-reading-group">https://ha
 nnes-stark.com/logag-reading-group</a></u><b><br></b><u></u><u></u><u></u><
 /html-blob>
LAST-MODIFIED:20250330T034656Z
LOCATION:https://zoom.us/j/5775722530?pwd=ZzlGTXlDNThhUDZOdU4vN2JRMm5pQT09
SEQUENCE:0
STATUS:CONFIRMED
SUMMARY:Learning on Graphs and Geometry Reading Group
TRANSP:OPAQUE
