BEGIN:VEVENT
DTSTART;TZID=Europe/Berlin:20240527T170000
DTEND;TZID=Europe/Berlin:20240527T180000
DTSTAMP:20260325T005103Z
ORGANIZER;CN=vdujn2pbpkhgr5f55cm39qbmu8@group.calendar.google.com:mailto:vd
 ujn2pbpkhgr5f55cm39qbmu8@group.calendar.google.com
UID:4om8dkr7eu2gvvk4o4mi4c9bjg_R20231211T160000@google.com
ATTENDEE;CUTYPE=INDIVIDUAL;ROLE=REQ-PARTICIPANT;PARTSTAT=ACCEPTED;CN=snirho
 rdan@gmail.com;X-NUM-GUESTS=0:mailto:snirhordan@gmail.com
ATTENDEE;CUTYPE=INDIVIDUAL;ROLE=REQ-PARTICIPANT;PARTSTAT=NEEDS-ACTION;CN=na
 davdym@technion.ac.il;X-NUM-GUESTS=0:mailto:nadavdym@technion.ac.il
RECURRENCE-ID;TZID=Europe/Berlin:20240527T170000
CREATED:20210724T162031Z
DESCRIPTION:<u></u><u></u><u></u><u></u><u></u><b>Speaker:</b><br><div dir=
 "auto">Snir Hordan who started his PhD in the Technion (Israel) about 6 mon
 ths ago\, supervised by Dr. Nadav Dym. He mainly looks at the expressivity 
 of WL tests applied to point clouds. <div dir="auto"><br><b>Paper:</b><br><
 b>Weisfeiler Leman for Euclidean Equivariant Machine Learning</b> <a href="
 https://arxiv.org/abs/2402.02484">https://arxiv.org/abs/2402.02484</a> (<a 
 href="https://arxiv.org/search/cs?searchtype=author&amp\;query=Hordan\,+S">
 <u>Snir Hordan</u></a>\, <a href="https://arxiv.org/search/cs?searchtype=au
 thor&amp\;query=Amir\,+T">Tal Amir</a>\, <a href="https://arxiv.org/search/
 cs?searchtype=author&amp\;query=Dym\,+N">Nadav Dym</a>)<br>The k-Weifeiler-
 Leman (k-WL) graph isomorphism test hierarchy is a common method for assess
 ing the expressive power of graph neural networks (GNNs). Recently\, the 2-
 WL test was proven to be complete on weighted graphs which encode 3D point 
 cloud data. Consequently\, GNNs whose expressive power is equivalent to the
  2-WL test are provably universal on point clouds. Yet\, this result is lim
 ited to invariant continuous functions on point clouds.<br>In this paper we
  extend this result in three ways: Firstly\, we show that 2-WL tests can be
  extended to point clouds which include both positions and velocity\, a sce
 nario often encountered in applications. Secondly\, we show that PPGN (Maro
 n et al.\, 2019) can simulate 2-WL uniformly on all point clouds with low c
 omplexity. Finally\, we show that a simple modification of this PPGN archit
 ecture can be used to obtain a universal equivariant architecture that can 
 approximate all continuous equivariant functions uniformly.<br>Building on 
 our results\, we develop our WeLNet architecture\, which can process positi
 on-velocity pairs\, compute functions fully equivariant to permutations and
  rigid motions\, and is provably complete and universal. Remarkably\, WeLNe
 t is provably complete precisely in the setting in which it is implemented 
 in practice. Our theoretical results are complemented by experiments showin
 g WeLNet sets new state-of-the-art results on the N-Body dynamics task and 
 the GEOM-QM9 molecular conformation generation task.<br><b><br></b><b>Meeti
 ng Details:<br></b><i>Every Monday at</i><i> </i><i>5pm CST / 4pm UTC / 11a
 m EST / 8am PST.  </i><br><a href="https://zoom.us/j/5775722530?pwd=ZzlGTXl
 DNThhUDZOdU4vN2JRMm5pQT09" target="_blank">https://zoom.us/j/5775722530?pwd
 =ZzlGTXlDNThhUDZOdU4vN2JRMm5pQT09</a><br><br><b>Slack Workspace for discuss
 ion and paper voting:<br></b><a href="https://join.slack.com/t/logag/shared
 _invite/zt-22y7n3k7a-FHwX31gc85yZCa0uF8BU7w" target="_blank">https://join.s
 lack.com/t/logag/shared_invite/zt-22y7n3k7a-FHwX31gc85yZCa0uF8BU7w</a><br><
 br><b>All information: Schedule of upcoming papers\, recordings\, mailing l
 ist</b><b>:<br></b><u><a href="https://hannes-stark.com/logag-reading-group
 " target="_blank">https://hannes-stark.com/logag-reading-group<br></a></u><
 b><br></b><u></u><u></u><u></u><u></u><u></u>
