UID:4om8dkr7eu2gvvk4o4mi4c9bjg_R20230327T150000@google.com
ATTENDEE;CUTYPE=INDIVIDUAL;ROLE=REQ-PARTICIPANT;PARTSTAT=NEEDS-ACTION;CN=je
 rome.henin@cnrs.fr;X-NUM-GUESTS=0:mailto:jerome.henin@cnrs.fr
ATTENDEE;CUTYPE=INDIVIDUAL;ROLE=REQ-PARTICIPANT;PARTSTAT=NEEDS-ACTION;CN=lo
 ris.felardos.212@use.startmail.com;X-NUM-GUESTS=0:mailto:loris.felardos.212
 @use.startmail.com
RECURRENCE-ID;TZID=Europe/Berlin:20231002T170000
CREATED:20210724T162031Z
DESCRIPTION:<u></u><u></u><u></u><u></u><u></u><b>Speaker:<br></b>Authors <
 a href="https://arxiv.org/search/cs?searchtype=author&amp\;query=Felardos%2
 C+L">Loris Felardos</a> and <a href="https://arxiv.org/search/cs?searchtype
 =author&amp\;query=H%C3%A9nin%2C+J">Jérôme Hénin</a>.<br><b><br></b><br><b>
 Paper:</b><br><b>Designing losses for data-free training of normalizing flo
 ws on Boltzmann distributions </b><a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2301.05475
 ">https://arxiv.org/abs/2301.05475</a> (<a href="https://arxiv.org/search/c
 s?searchtype=author&amp\;query=Felardos%2C+L"><u>Loris Felardos</u></a> (TA
 U)\, <a href="https://arxiv.org/search/cs?searchtype=author&amp\;query=H%C3
 %A9nin%2C+J">Jérôme Hénin</a> (LBT (UPR\\_9080)\, IBPC (FR\\_550))\, <a hre
 f="https://arxiv.org/search/cs?searchtype=author&amp\;query=Charpiat%2C+G">
 Guillaume Charpiat</a> (TAU))<br>Generating a Boltzmann distribution in hig
 h dimension has recently been achieved with Normalizing Flows\, which enabl
 e fast and exact computation of the generated density\, and thus unbiased e
 stimation of expectations. However\, current implementations rely on accura
 te training data\, which typically comes from computationally expensive sim
 ulations. There is therefore a clear incentive to train models with incompl
 ete or no data by relying solely on the target density\, which can be obtai
 ned from a physical energy model (up to a constant factor). For that purpos
 e\, we analyze the properties of standard losses based on Kullback-Leibler 
 divergences. We showcase their limitations\, in particular a strong propens
 ity for mode collapse during optimization on high-dimensional distributions
 . We then propose strategies to alleviate these issues\, most importantly a
  new loss function well-grounded in theory and with suitable optimization p
 roperties. Using as a benchmark the generation of 3D molecular configuratio
 ns\, we show on several tasks that\, for the first time\, imperfect pre-tra
 ined models can be further optimized in the absence of training data.<br><b
 ><br><br></b><b>Meeting Details:<br></b><i>Every Monday at</i><i> </i><i>5p
 m CEST / 3pm UTC / 11am EDT / 8am PDT.  </i><br><a href="https://zoom.us/j/
 5775722530?pwd=ZzlGTXlDNThhUDZOdU4vN2JRMm5pQT09">https://zoom.us/j/57757225
 30?pwd=ZzlGTXlDNThhUDZOdU4vN2JRMm5pQT09</a><br><br><b>Slack Workspace for d
 iscussion and paper voting:<br></b><a href="https://join.slack.com/t/logag/
 shared_invite/zt-1hcqybjyd-ty5Pbj~BQy~W4B4mQQVxDQ">https://join.slack.com/t
 /logag/shared_invite/zt-1hcqybjyd-ty5Pbj~BQy~W4B4mQQVxDQ</a><br><br><b>All 
 information: Schedule of upcoming papers\, recordings\, mailing list</b><b>
 :<br></b><u><a href="https://hannes-stark.com/logag-reading-group">https://
 hannes-stark.com/logag-reading-group</a></u><b><br></b><u></u><u></u><u></u
 ><u></u><u></u>
