BEGIN:VEVENT
DTSTART;TZID=Europe/Berlin:20240129T170000
DTEND;TZID=Europe/Berlin:20240129T180000
DTSTAMP:20260325T005103Z
ORGANIZER;CN=vdujn2pbpkhgr5f55cm39qbmu8@group.calendar.google.com:mailto:vd
 ujn2pbpkhgr5f55cm39qbmu8@group.calendar.google.com
UID:4om8dkr7eu2gvvk4o4mi4c9bjg_R20231211T160000@google.com
ATTENDEE;CUTYPE=INDIVIDUAL;ROLE=REQ-PARTICIPANT;PARTSTAT=ACCEPTED;CN=vdujn2
 pbpkhgr5f55cm39qbmu8@group.calendar.google.com;X-NUM-GUESTS=0:mailto:vdujn2
 pbpkhgr5f55cm39qbmu8@group.calendar.google.com
ATTENDEE;CUTYPE=INDIVIDUAL;ROLE=REQ-PARTICIPANT;PARTSTAT=NEEDS-ACTION;CN=na
 mkyeong96@kaist.ac.kr;X-NUM-GUESTS=0:mailto:namkyeong96@kaist.ac.kr
RECURRENCE-ID;TZID=Europe/Berlin:20240129T170000
CREATED:20210724T162031Z
DESCRIPTION:<u></u><u></u><u></u><u></u><u></u><b>Speaker:<br></b><br>Namky
 eong Lee\,<span> Ph.D student in KAIST\, and currently visiting scholar in 
 UIUC under the supervision of Prof. Jimeng Sun.</span><br><b>Paper:</b><br>
 <b>Conditional Graph Information Bottleneck for Molecular Relational Learni
 ng (</b><a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2305.01520">https://arxiv.org/abs/23
 05.01520</a> (<a href="https://arxiv.org/search/q-bio?searchtype=author&amp
 \;query=Lee\,+N">Namkyeong Lee</a>\, <a href="https://arxiv.org/search/q-bi
 o?searchtype=author&amp\;query=Hyun\,+D">Dongmin Hyun</a>\, <a href="https:
 //arxiv.org/search/q-bio?searchtype=author&amp\;query=Na\,+G+S">Gyoung S. N
 a</a>\, <a href="https://arxiv.org/search/q-bio?searchtype=author&amp\;quer
 y=Kim\,+S">Sungwon Kim</a>\, <a href="https://arxiv.org/search/q-bio?search
 type=author&amp\;query=Lee\,+J">Junseok Lee</a>\, <a href="https://arxiv.or
 g/search/q-bio?searchtype=author&amp\;query=Park\,+C">Chanyoung Park</a>)<b
 r>Molecular relational learning\, whose goal is to learn the interaction be
 havior between molecular pairs\, got a surge of interest in molecular scien
 ces due to its wide range of applications. Recently\, graph neural networks
  have recently shown great success in molecular relational learning by mode
 ling a molecule as a graph structure\, and considering atom-level interacti
 ons between two molecules. Despite their success\, existing molecular relat
 ional learning methods tend to overlook the nature of chemistry\, i.e.\, a 
 chemical compound is composed of multiple substructures such as functional 
 groups that cause distinctive chemical reactions. In this work\, we propose
  a novel relational learning framework\, called CGIB\, that predicts the in
 teraction behavior between a pair of graphs by detecting core subgraphs the
 rein. The main idea is\, given a pair of graphs\, to find a subgraph from a
  graph that contains the minimal sufficient information regarding the task 
 at hand conditioned on the paired graph based on the principle of condition
 al graph information bottleneck. We argue that our proposed method mimics t
 he nature of chemical reactions\, i.e.\, the core substructure of a molecul
 e varies depending on which other molecule it interacts with. Extensive exp
 eriments on various tasks with real-world datasets demonstrate the superior
 ity of CGIB over state-of-the-art baselines. Our code is available at this 
 https URL.<br><b><br></b><b>Meeting Details:<br></b><i>Every Monday at</i><
 i> </i><i>5pm CST / 4pm UTC / 11am EST / 8am PST.  </i><br><a href="https:/
 /zoom.us/j/5775722530?pwd=ZzlGTXlDNThhUDZOdU4vN2JRMm5pQT09">https://zoom.us
 /j/5775722530?pwd=ZzlGTXlDNThhUDZOdU4vN2JRMm5pQT09</a><br><br><b>Slack Work
 space for discussion and paper voting:<br></b><a href="https://join.slack.c
 om/t/logag/shared_invite/zt-22y7n3k7a-FHwX31gc85yZCa0uF8BU7w">https://join.
 slack.com/t/logag/shared_invite/zt-22y7n3k7a-FHwX31gc85yZCa0uF8BU7w</a><br>
 <br><b>All information: Schedule of upcoming papers\, recordings\, mailing 
 list</b><b>:<br></b><u><a href="https://hannes-stark.com/logag-reading-grou
 p">https://hannes-stark.com/logag-reading-group</a></u><b><br></b><u></u><u
 ></u><u></u><u></u><u></u>
LAST-MODIFIED:20240904T130202Z
LOCATION:https://zoom.us/j/5775722530?pwd=ZzlGTXlDNThhUDZOdU4vN2JRMm5pQT09
