BEGIN:VEVENT
DTSTART;TZID=Europe/Berlin:20230807T170000
DTEND;TZID=Europe/Berlin:20230807T180000
DTSTAMP:20260325T005103Z
ORGANIZER;CN=vdujn2pbpkhgr5f55cm39qbmu8@group.calendar.google.com:mailto:vd
 ujn2pbpkhgr5f55cm39qbmu8@group.calendar.google.com
UID:4om8dkr7eu2gvvk4o4mi4c9bjg_R20230327T150000@google.com
ATTENDEE;CUTYPE=INDIVIDUAL;ROLE=REQ-PARTICIPANT;PARTSTAT=NEEDS-ACTION;CN=pi
 @kimlab.org;X-NUM-GUESTS=0:mailto:pi@kimlab.org
ATTENDEE;CUTYPE=INDIVIDUAL;ROLE=REQ-PARTICIPANT;PARTSTAT=NEEDS-ACTION;CN=os
 ama.abdin@mail.utoronto.ca;X-NUM-GUESTS=0:mailto:osama.abdin@mail.utoronto.
 ca
RECURRENCE-ID;TZID=Europe/Berlin:20230807T170000
CREATED:20210724T162031Z
DESCRIPTION:<u></u><u></u><u></u><u></u><u></u><b>Speaker:<br></b>First aut
 hor Osama Abdin from University of Toronto.<br><br><b>Paper:</b><br><b>PepF
 low: direct conformational sampling from peptide energy landscapes through 
 hypernetwork-conditioned diffusion </b><a href="https://www.biorxiv.org/con
 tent/10.1101/2023.06.25.546443v1">https://www.biorxiv.org/content/10.1101/2
 023.06.25.546443v1</a> <b>(</b><span>Osama</span><span> </span><span>Abdin<
 /span><span>\,</span><span> </span><span>Philip M.</span><span> </span><spa
 n>Kim)</span><b><br></b>Deep learning approaches have spurred substantial a
 dvances in the single-state prediction of biomolecular structures. The func
 tion of biomolecules is\, however\, dependent on the range of conformations
  they can assume. This is especially true for peptides\, a highly flexible 
 class of molecules that are involved in numerous biological processes and a
 re of high interest as therapeutics. Here\, we introduce PepFlow\, a genera
 lized Boltzmann generator that enables direct all-atom sampling from the al
 lowable conformational space of input peptides. We train the model in a dif
 fusion framework and subsequently use an equivalent flow to perform conform
 ational sampling. To overcome the prohibitive cost of generalized all-atom 
 modelling\, we modularize the generation process and integrate a hypernetwo
 rk to predict sequence-specific network parameters. PepFlow accurately pred
 icts peptide structures and effectively recapitulates experimental peptide 
 ensembles at a fraction of the running time of traditional approaches. PepF
 low can additionally be used to sample conformations that satisfy constrain
 ts such as macrocyclization.<br><b><br></b><b>Meeting Details:<br></b><i>Ev
 ery Monday at</i><i> </i><i>5pm CEST / 3pm UTC / 11am EDT / 8am PDT.  </i><
 br><a href="https://zoom.us/j/5775722530?pwd=ZzlGTXlDNThhUDZOdU4vN2JRMm5pQT
 09">https://zoom.us/j/5775722530?pwd=ZzlGTXlDNThhUDZOdU4vN2JRMm5pQT09</a><b
 r><br><b>Slack Workspace for discussion and paper voting:<br></b><a href="h
 ttps://join.slack.com/t/logag/shared_invite/zt-1hcqybjyd-ty5Pbj~BQy~W4B4mQQ
 VxDQ">https://join.slack.com/t/logag/shared_invite/zt-1hcqybjyd-ty5Pbj~BQy~
 W4B4mQQVxDQ</a><br><br><b>All information: Schedule of upcoming papers\, re
 cordings\, mailing list</b><b>:<br></b><u><a href="https://hannes-stark.com
 /logag-reading-group">https://hannes-stark.com/logag-reading-group</a></u><
 b><br></b><u></u><u></u><u></u><u></u><u></u>
LAST-MODIFIED:20240904T130202Z
