BEGIN:VEVENT
DTSTART;TZID=Europe/Berlin:20210928T170000
DTEND;TZID=Europe/Berlin:20210928T180000
DTSTAMP:20260325T005103Z
ORGANIZER;CN=vdujn2pbpkhgr5f55cm39qbmu8@group.calendar.google.com:mailto:vd
 ujn2pbpkhgr5f55cm39qbmu8@group.calendar.google.com
UID:1hfd2s2r4hmta9og6idelbrfic_R20210824T150000@google.com
ATTENDEE;CUTYPE=INDIVIDUAL;ROLE=REQ-PARTICIPANT;PARTSTAT=NEEDS-ACTION;CN=ko
 ng@cs.umd.edu;X-NUM-GUESTS=0:mailto:kong@cs.umd.edu
ATTENDEE;CUTYPE=INDIVIDUAL;ROLE=REQ-PARTICIPANT;PARTSTAT=ACCEPTED;CN=kong@u
 md.edu;X-NUM-GUESTS=0:mailto:kong@umd.edu
RECURRENCE-ID;TZID=Europe/Berlin:20210928T170000
CREATED:20210724T162031Z
DESCRIPTION:<b>Speaker:<br></b>First author of the paper&nbsp\;<a href="htt
 ps://scholar.google.com/citations?user=MG46jrMAAAAJ&hl=en">Kezhi Kong</a>&n
 bsp\;who is a PhD student at the University of Maryland. Advised by Prof. T
 om Goldstein\, he does research in Machine Learning\, with a focus on Graph
  Learning and Adversarial Attacks/Defenses.<br><br><b>Paper:</b><br><b>FLAG
 : Adversarial Data Augmentation for Graph Neural Networks&nbsp\;</b><a href
 ="https://arxiv.org/abs/2010.09891">https://arxiv.org/abs/2010.09891</a>&nb
 sp\;(<a href="https://arxiv.org/search/cs?searchtype=author&amp\;query=Kong
 %2C+K">Kezhi Kong</a>\,&nbsp\;<a href="https://arxiv.org/search/cs?searchty
 pe=author&amp\;query=Li%2C+G">Guohao Li</a>\,&nbsp\;<a href="https://arxiv.
 org/search/cs?searchtype=author&amp\;query=Ding%2C+M">Mucong Ding</a>\,&nbs
 p\;<a href="https://arxiv.org/search/cs?searchtype=author&amp\;query=Wu%2C+
 Z">Zuxuan Wu</a>\,&nbsp\;<a href="https://arxiv.org/search/cs?searchtype=au
 thor&amp\;query=Zhu%2C+C">Chen Zhu</a>\,&nbsp\;<a href="https://arxiv.org/s
 earch/cs?searchtype=author&amp\;query=Ghanem%2C+B">Bernard Ghanem</a>\,&nbs
 p\;<a href="https://arxiv.org/search/cs?searchtype=author&amp\;query=Taylor
 %2C+G">Gavin Taylor</a>\,&nbsp\;<a href="https://arxiv.org/search/cs?search
 type=author&amp\;query=Goldstein%2C+T">Tom Goldstein</a>)<br>Data augmentat
 ion helps neural networks generalize better\, but it remains an open questi
 on how to effectively augment graph data to enhance the performance of GNNs
  (Graph Neural Networks). While most existing graph regularizers focus on a
 ugmenting graph topological structures by adding/removing edges\, we offer 
 a novel direction to augment in the input node feature space for better per
 formance. We propose a simple but effective solution\, FLAG (Free Large-sca
 le Adversarial Augmentation on Graphs)\, which iteratively augments node fe
 atures with gradient-based adversarial perturbations during training\, and 
 boosts performance at test time. Empirically\, FLAG can be easily implement
 ed with a dozen lines of code and is flexible enough to function with any G
 NN backbone\, on a wide variety of large-scale datasets\, and in both trans
 ductive and inductive settings. Without modifying a model's architecture or
  training setup\, FLAG yields a consistent and salient performance boost ac
 ross both node and graph classification tasks. Using FLAG\, we reach state-
 of-the-art performance on the large-scale ogbg-molpcba\, ogbg-ppa\, and ogb
 g-code datasets.<br><b><br></b><b>Meeting Details:<br></b><i>Every Tuesday 
 at 5pm CEST / 3pm UTC / 11am EST / 8am PST</i><br><a href="https://us02web.
 zoom.us/j/4556095405?pwd=UmZRbC95d050TkRsZ2QvWER3SDdqUT09">https://us02web.
 zoom.us/j/4556095405?pwd=UmZRbC95d050TkRsZ2QvWER3SDdqUT09</a><br>Meeting ID
 : 455 609 5405<br>Passcode: logag<br><br><b>Join Slack Workspace for discus
 sions beyond the meetings and paper voting:<br></b><a href="https://join.sl
 ack.com/t/logag/shared_invite/zt-u0mbo1ec-zElmvd1oSCXGjXvxLSokvg">https://j
 oin.slack.com/t/<u></u>logag/shared_invite/zt-<u></u>u0mbo1ec-<u></u>zElmvd
 1oSCXGjXvxLSokvg</a><br><br><b>All information: Schedule of upcoming talks\
 , slides + notes of previously covered papers</b><b>:<br></b><a href="http:
 ///">https://hannes-stark.com/logag-reading-group</a><b><br></b>
LAST-MODIFIED:20250330T034906Z
SEQUENCE:0
STATUS:CONFIRMED
SUMMARY:Learning on Graphs and Geometry Reading Group
TRANSP:OPAQUE
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
DTSTART;TZID=Europe/Berlin:20211026T170000
DTEND;TZID=Europe/Berlin:20211026T180000
DTSTAMP:20260325T005103Z
ORGANIZER;CN=vdujn2pbpkhgr5f55cm39qbmu8@group.calendar.google.com:mailto:vd
 ujn2pbpkhgr5f55cm39qbmu8@group.calendar.google.com
UID:1hfd2s2r4hmta9og6idelbrfic_R20210824T150000@google.com
ATTENDEE;CUTYPE=INDIVIDUAL;ROLE=REQ-PARTICIPANT;PARTSTAT=NEEDS-ACTION;CN=ja
 browbottom@gmail.com;X-NUM-GUESTS=0:mailto:jabrowbottom@gmail.com
ATTENDEE;CUTYPE=INDIVIDUAL;ROLE=REQ-PARTICIPANT;PARTSTAT=ACCEPTED;CN=bchamb
 erlain@twitter.com;X-NUM-GUESTS=0:mailto:bchamberlain@twitter.com
RECURRENCE-ID;TZID=Europe/Berlin:20211026T170000
CREATED:20210724T162031Z
DESCRIPTION:<b>Speaker:<br></b>Joint first authors of the paper&nbsp\;<a hr
 ef="https://www.linkedin.com/in/jamesrowbottom/?originalSubdomain=uk">James
  Rowbottom</a>&nbsp\;and Dr.&nbsp\;<a href="https://www.linkedin.com/in/ben
 jaminchamberlain/">Benjamin Paul Chamberlain</a>. James received his master
  in AI with distinction from Imperial College London and worked on GRAND as
  an ML Reserch intern at Prof.&nbsp\;<a href="https://www.imperial.ac.uk/pe
 ople/m.bronstein">Michael Bronstein's</a>&nbsp\;graph ML research group at 
 Twitter. Ben is a Machine Learning Researcher at Twitter who received his P
 hD from Imperial College London.<br><br><b>Papers:</b><br><b>GRAND: Graph N
 eural Diffusion</b>&nbsp\;<a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2106.10934">https:
 //arxiv.org/abs/2106.10934</a>&nbsp\;(<a href="https://arxiv.org/search/cs?
 searchtype=author&query=Chamberlain%2C+B+P">Benjamin Paul Chamberlain</a>\,
 &nbsp\;<a href="https://arxiv.org/search/cs?searchtype=author&query=Rowbott
 om%2C+J">James Rowbottom</a>\,&nbsp\;<a href="https://arxiv.org/search/cs?s
 earchtype=author&query=Gorinova%2C+M">Maria Gorinova</a>\,&nbsp\;<a href="h
 ttps://arxiv.org/search/cs?searchtype=author&query=Webb%2C+S">Stefan Webb</
 a>\,&nbsp\;<a href="https://arxiv.org/search/cs?searchtype=author&query=Ros
 si%2C+E">Emanuele Rossi</a>\,&nbsp\;<a href="https://arxiv.org/search/cs?se
 archtype=author&query=Bronstein%2C+M+M">Michael M. Bronstein</a>)<br>We pre
 sent Graph Neural Diffusion (GRAND) that approaches deep learning on graphs
  as a continuous diffusion process and treats Graph Neural Networks (GNNs) 
 as discretisations of an underlying PDE. In our model\, the layer structure
  and topology correspond to the discretisation choices of temporal and spat
 ial operators. Our approach<br>&nbsp\;allows a principled development of a 
 broad new class of GNNs that are able to address the common plights of grap
 h learning models such as depth\, oversmoothing\, and bottlenecks. Key to t
 he success of our models are stability with respect to perturbations in the
  data and this is addressed for both implicit and explicit discretisation s
 chemes. We develop linear and nonlinear versions of GRAND\, which achieve c
 ompetitive results on many standard graph benchmarks.<br><b>Beltrami Flow a
 nd Neural Diffusion on Graphs&nbsp\;</b><a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2110
 .09443">https://arxiv.org/abs/2110.09443</a><b>&nbsp\;(</b><a href="https:/
 /arxiv.org/search/cs?searchtype=author&query=Chamberlain%2C+B+P">Benjamin P
 aul Chamberlain</a>\,&nbsp\;<a href="https://arxiv.org/search/cs?searchtype
 =author&query=Rowbottom%2C+J">James Rowbottom</a>\,&nbsp\;<a href="https://
 arxiv.org/search/cs?searchtype=author&query=Eynard%2C+D">Davide Eynard</a>\
 ,&nbsp\;<a href="https://arxiv.org/search/cs?searchtype=author&query=Di+Gio
 vanni%2C+F">Francesco Di Giovanni</a>\,&nbsp\;<a href="https://arxiv.org/se
 arch/cs?searchtype=author&query=Dong%2C+X">Xiaowen Dong</a>\,&nbsp\;<a href
 ="https://arxiv.org/search/cs?searchtype=author&query=Bronstein%2C+M+M">Mic
 hael M Bronstein</a>)<br>We propose a novel class of graph neural networks 
 based on the discretised Beltrami flow\, a non-Euclidean diffusion PDE. In 
 our model\, node features are supplemented with positional encodings derive
 d from the graph topology and jointly evolved by the Beltrami flow\, produc
 ing simultaneously continuous feature learning and topology evolution. The 
 resulting model generalises many popular graph neural networks and achieves
  state-of-the-art results on several benchmarks.<br><b><br></b><b>Meeting D
 etails:<br></b><i>Every Tuesday at 5pm CEST / 3pm UTC / 11am EST / 8am PST<
 /i><br><a href="https://us02web.zoom.us/j/4556095405?pwd=UmZRbC95d050TkRsZ2
 QvWER3SDdqUT09">https://us02web.zoom.us/j/4556095405?pwd=UmZRbC95d050TkRsZ2
 QvWER3SDdqUT09</a><br><br><b>Join Slack Workspace for discussions beyond th
 e meetings and paper voting:<br></b><a href="https://join.slack.com/t/logag
 /shared_invite/zt-u0mbo1ec-zElmvd1oSCXGjXvxLSokvg">https://join.slack.com/t
 /<u></u>logag/shared_invite/zt-<u></u>u0mbo1ec-<u></u>zElmvd1oSCXGjXvxLSokv
 g</a><br><br><b>All information: Schedule of upcoming talks\, slides + note
 s of previously covered papers</b><b>:<br></b><a href="https://hannes-stark
 .com/logag-reading-group" id="ow696" __is_owner="true">https://hannes-stark
 .com/logag-reading-group</a><b><br></b>
