CREATED:20210724T162031Z
DESCRIPTION:<u></u><u></u><u></u><u></u><u></u><b>Speaker:<br></b>Shuxin Zh
 eng obtained Ph.D degree from University of Science and Technology of China
 \, and joined Microsoft Research as a Senior Researcher. He is dedicated to
  researching AI algorithms and exploring scientific fields using artificial
  intelligence. He developed the molecular modeling algorithm Graphormer\, w
 hich has won multiple international competitions in AI molecular modeling a
 nd molecular dynamics simulation\, defeating teams from DeepMind and Facebo
 ok AI Research.<br><br><b>Paper:</b><br><b>Towards Predicting Equilibrium D
 istributions for Molecular Systems with Deep Learning </b><a href="https://
 arxiv.org/abs/2306.05445">https://arxiv.org/abs/2306.05445</a> (<a href="ht
 tps://arxiv.org/search/physics?searchtype=author&amp\;query=Zheng%2C+S"><u>
 Shuxin Zheng</u></a>\, <a href="https://arxiv.org/search/physics?searchtype
 =author&amp\;query=He%2C+J">Jiyan He</a>\, <a href="https://arxiv.org/searc
 h/physics?searchtype=author&amp\;query=Liu%2C+C">Chang Liu</a>\, <a href="h
 ttps://arxiv.org/search/physics?searchtype=author&amp\;query=Shi%2C+Y">Yu S
 hi</a>\, <a href="https://arxiv.org/search/physics?searchtype=author&amp\;q
 uery=Lu%2C+Z">Ziheng Lu</a>\, <a href="https://arxiv.org/search/physics?sea
 rchtype=author&amp\;query=Feng%2C+W">Weitao Feng</a>\, <a href="https://arx
 iv.org/search/physics?searchtype=author&amp\;query=Ju%2C+F">Fusong Ju</a>\,
  <a href="https://arxiv.org/search/physics?searchtype=author&amp\;query=Wan
 g%2C+J">Jiaxi Wang</a>\, <a href="https://arxiv.org/search/physics?searchty
 pe=author&amp\;query=Zhu%2C+J">Jianwei Zhu</a>\, <a href="https://arxiv.org
 /search/physics?searchtype=author&amp\;query=Min%2C+Y">Yaosen Min</a>\, <a 
 href="https://arxiv.org/search/physics?searchtype=author&amp\;query=Zhang%2
 C+H">He Zhang</a>\, <a href="https://arxiv.org/search/physics?searchtype=au
 thor&amp\;query=Tang%2C+S">Shidi Tang</a>\, <a href="https://arxiv.org/sear
 ch/physics?searchtype=author&amp\;query=Hao%2C+H">Hongxia Hao</a>\, <a href
 ="https://arxiv.org/search/physics?searchtype=author&amp\;query=Jin%2C+P">P
 eiran Jin</a>\, <a href="https://arxiv.org/search/physics?searchtype=author
 &amp\;query=Chen%2C+C">Chi Chen</a>\, <a href="https://arxiv.org/search/phy
 sics?searchtype=author&amp\;query=No%C3%A9%2C+F">Frank Noé</a>\, <a href="h
 ttps://arxiv.org/search/physics?searchtype=author&amp\;query=Liu%2C+H">Haig
 uang Liu</a>\, <a href="https://arxiv.org/search/physics?searchtype=author&
 amp\;query=Liu%2C+T">Tie-Yan Liu</a>)<br>Advances in deep learning have gre
 atly improved structure prediction of molecules. However\, many macroscopic
  observations that are important for real-world applications are not functi
 ons of a single molecular structure\, but rather determined from the equili
 brium distribution of structures. Traditional methods for obtaining these d
 istributions\, such as molecular dynamics simulation\, are computationally 
 expensive and often intractable. In this paper\, we introduce a novel deep 
 learning framework\, called Distributional Graphormer (DiG)\, in an attempt
  to predict the equilibrium distribution of molecular systems. Inspired by 
 the annealing process in thermodynamics\, DiG employs deep neural networks 
 to transform a simple distribution towards the equilibrium distribution\, c
 onditioned on a descriptor of a molecular system\, such as a chemical graph
  or a protein sequence. This framework enables efficient generation of dive
 rse conformations and provides estimations of state densities. We demonstra
 te the performance of DiG on several molecular tasks\, including protein co
 nformation sampling\, ligand structure sampling\, catalyst-adsorbate sampli
 ng\, and property-guided structure generation. DiG presents a significant a
 dvancement in methodology for statistically understanding molecular systems
 \, opening up new research opportunities in molecular science.<br><b><br></
 b><b>Meeting Details:<br></b><i>Every Monday at</i><i> </i><i>5pm CEST / 3p
 m UTC / 11am EDT / 8am PDT.  </i><br><a href="https://zoom.us/j/5775722530?
 pwd=ZzlGTXlDNThhUDZOdU4vN2JRMm5pQT09">https://zoom.us/j/5775722530?pwd=ZzlG
 TXlDNThhUDZOdU4vN2JRMm5pQT09</a><br><br><b>Slack Workspace for discussion a
 nd paper voting:<br></b><a href="https://join.slack.com/t/logag/shared_invi
 te/zt-1hcqybjyd-ty5Pbj~BQy~W4B4mQQVxDQ">https://join.slack.com/t/logag/shar
 ed_invite/zt-1hcqybjyd-ty5Pbj~BQy~W4B4mQQVxDQ</a><br><br><b>All information
 : Schedule of upcoming papers\, recordings\, mailing list</b><b>:<br></b><u
 ><a href="https://hannes-stark.com/logag-reading-group">https://hannes-star
 k.com/logag-reading-group</a></u><b><br></b><u></u><u></u><u></u><u></u><u>
 </u>
