DESCRIPTION:<html-blob><u></u><u></u><b>Speaker:<br></b>Professor <a href="
 http://nimadehmamy.com/">Nima Dehmamy</a>&nbsp\;who works at Northwestern U
 niversity and earned his PhD in Physics at Boston University. His&nbsp\;res
 earch involves AI in graph learning\, using physics to understand optimizat
 ion landscapes\, and neuroscience.<br><br><b>Paper:</b><br><b>Automatic Sym
 metry Discovery with Lie Algebra Convolutional Network</b>&nbsp\;<a href="h
 ttps://arxiv.org/abs/2109.07103" class="paper-link" style="display: inline-
 block\;">https://arxiv.org/abs/2109.07103</a> (<a href="https://arxiv.org/s
 earch/cs?searchtype=author&query=Dehmamy%2C+N">Nima Dehmamy</a>\,&nbsp\;<a 
 href="https://arxiv.org/search/cs?searchtype=author&query=Walters%2C+R">Rob
 in Walters</a>\,&nbsp\;<a href="https://arxiv.org/search/cs?searchtype=auth
 or&query=Liu%2C+Y">Yanchen Liu</a>\,&nbsp\;<a href="https://arxiv.org/searc
 h/cs?searchtype=author&query=Wang%2C+D">Dashun Wang</a>\,&nbsp\;<a href="ht
 tps://arxiv.org/search/cs?searchtype=author&query=Yu%2C+R">Rose Yu</a>) 202
 1<br>Existing equivariant neural networks require prior knowledge of the sy
 mmetry group and discretization for continuous groups. We propose to work w
 ith Lie algebras (infinitesimal generators) instead of Lie groups. Our mode
 l\, the Lie algebra convolutional network (L-conv) can automatically discov
 er symmetries and does not require discretization of the group. We show tha
 t L-conv can serve as a building block to construct any group equivariant f
 eedforward architecture. Both CNNs and Graph Convolutional Networks can be 
 expressed as L-conv with appropriate groups. We discover direct connections
  between L-conv and physics: (1) group invariant loss generalizes field the
 ory (2) Euler-Lagrange equation measures the robustness\, and (3) equivaria
 nce leads to conservation laws and Noether current.These connections open u
 p new avenues for designing more general equivariant networks and applying 
 them to important problems in physical sciences<br><b><br></b><b>Meeting De
 tails:<br></b><i>Every Tuesday at</i><i> </i><i>5pm CEST / 3pm UTC / 11am E
 ST / 8am PDT.&nbsp\;&nbsp\;</i><br><a href="https://zoom.us/j/5775722530?pw
 d=ZzlGTXlDNThhUDZOdU4vN2JRMm5pQT09">https://zoom.us/j/5775722530?pwd=ZzlGTX
 lDNThhUDZOdU4vN2JRMm5pQT09</a><br><br><b>Slack Workspace for discussion and
  paper voting:<br></b><a href="https://join.slack.com/t/logag/shared_invite
 /zt-u0mbo1ec-zElmvd1oSCXGjXvxLSokvg">https://join.slack.com/t/<u></u>logag/
 shared_invite/zt-<u></u>u0mbo1ec-<u></u>zElmvd1oSCXGjXvxLSokvg</a><br><br><
 b>All information: Schedule of upcoming papers\, recordings\, mailing list<
 /b><b>:<br></b><u><a href="https://hannes-stark.com/logag-reading-group">ht
 tps://hannes-stark.com/logag-reading-group</a></u><b><br></b><u></u><u></u>
 </html-blob>\n\n-::~:~::~:~:~:~:~:~:~:~:~:~:~:~:~:~:~:~:~:~:~:~:~:~:~:~:~:~
