ORGANIZER;CN=vdujn2pbpkhgr5f55cm39qbmu8@group.calendar.google.com:mailto:vd
 ujn2pbpkhgr5f55cm39qbmu8@group.calendar.google.com
UID:4om8dkr7eu2gvvk4o4mi4c9bjg_R20230327T150000@google.com
ATTENDEE;CUTYPE=INDIVIDUAL;ROLE=REQ-PARTICIPANT;PARTSTAT=NEEDS-ACTION;CN=so
 ojungy@mit.edu;X-NUM-GUESTS=0:mailto:soojungy@mit.edu
RECURRENCE-ID;TZID=Europe/Berlin:20230410T170000
CREATED:20210724T162031Z
DESCRIPTION:<u></u><u></u><u></u><u></u><u></u><b>Speaker:<br></b><a href="
 https://sites.google.com/view/soojungy/">Soojung</a> is a PhD student from 
 Prof. Rafael Gomez-Bombarelli’s group at Department of Materials Science an
 d Engineering\, Massachusetts Institute of Technology. She is interested in
  modeling molecular interactions and dynamics by leveraging computational c
 hemistry simulations and geometric deep learning. Her current and previous 
 projects include protein-ligand docking\, protein-binding molecule generati
 on\, and generative reverse mapping of coarse-grained proteins.<br><br><b>P
 aper:</b><br><b>Chemically Transferable Generative Backmapping of Coarse-Gr
 ained Proteins </b><a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2303.01569">https://arxiv
 .org/abs/2303.01569</a> (<a href="https://arxiv.org/search/cs?searchtype=au
 thor&amp\;query=Yang%2C+S">Soojung Yang</a>\, <a href="https://arxiv.org/se
 arch/cs?searchtype=author&amp\;query=G%C3%B3mez-Bombarelli%2C+R">Rafael Góm
 ez-Bombarelli</a>)<br>Coarse-graining (CG) accelerates molecular simulation
 s of protein dynamics by simulating sets of atoms as singular beads. Backma
 pping is the opposite operation of bringing lost atomistic details back fro
 m the CG representation. While machine learning (ML) has produced accurate 
 and efficient CG simulations of proteins\, fast and reliable backmapping re
 mains a challenge. Rule-based methods produce poor all-atom geometries\, ne
 eding computationally costly refinement through additional simulations. Rec
 ently proposed ML approaches outperform traditional baselines but are not t
 ransferable between proteins and sometimes generate unphysical atom placeme
 nts with steric clashes and implausible torsion angles. This work addresses
  both issues to build a fast\, transferable\, and reliable generative backm
 apping tool for CG protein representations. We achieve generalization and r
 eliability through a combined set of innovations: representation based on i
 nternal coordinates\; an equivariant encoder/prior\; a custom loss function
  that helps ensure local structure\, global structure\, and physical constr
 aints\; and expert curation of high-quality out-of-equilibrium protein data
  for training. Our results pave the way for out-of-the-box backmapping of c
 oarse-grained simulations for arbitrary proteins.<b><br><br></b><b>Meeting 
 Details:<br></b><i>Every Monday at</i><i> </i><i>5pm CEST / 3pm UTC / 11am 
 EDT / 8am PDT.  </i><br><a href="https://zoom.us/j/5775722530?pwd=ZzlGTXlDN
 ThhUDZOdU4vN2JRMm5pQT09">https://zoom.us/j/5775722530?pwd=ZzlGTXlDNThhUDZOd
 U4vN2JRMm5pQT09</a><br><br><b>Slack Workspace for discussion and paper voti
 ng:<br></b><a href="https://join.slack.com/t/logag/shared_invite/zt-1hcqybj
 yd-ty5Pbj~BQy~W4B4mQQVxDQ">https://join.slack.com/t/logag/shared_invite/zt-
 1hcqybjyd-ty5Pbj~BQy~W4B4mQQVxDQ</a><br><br><b>All information: Schedule of
  upcoming papers\, recordings\, mailing list</b><b>:<br></b><u><a href="htt
 ps://hannes-stark.com/logag-reading-group">https://hannes-stark.com/logag-r
 eading-group</a></u><b><br></b><u></u><u></u><u></u><u></u><u></u>
