DTEND;TZID=Europe/Berlin:20250728T190000
DTSTAMP:20260325T005103Z
ORGANIZER;CN=vdujn2pbpkhgr5f55cm39qbmu8@group.calendar.google.com:mailto:vd
 ujn2pbpkhgr5f55cm39qbmu8@group.calendar.google.com
UID:b1qril1ke5kft3h5gbg6tqm1ob_R20250630T160000@google.com
ATTENDEE;CUTYPE=INDIVIDUAL;ROLE=REQ-PARTICIPANT;PARTSTAT=ACCEPTED;CN=pranam
 @seas.upenn.edu;X-NUM-GUESTS=0:mailto:pranam@seas.upenn.edu
ATTENDEE;CUTYPE=INDIVIDUAL;ROLE=REQ-PARTICIPANT;PARTSTAT=ACCEPTED;CN=sophta
 ng@seas.upenn.edu;X-NUM-GUESTS=0:mailto:sophtang@seas.upenn.edu
RECURRENCE-ID;TZID=Europe/Berlin:20250728T180000
CREATED:20210724T162031Z
DESCRIPTION:<u></u><u></u><u></u><u></u><u></u><b>Speaker:<br></b>Sophia Ta
 ng from Pranam Chatterjee's group at UPenn.<br><br><b>Paper:</b><br><b>Bran
 ched Schrödinger Bridge Matching </b><a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2506.09
 007">https://arxiv.org/abs/2506.09007</a> (<a href="https://arxiv.org/searc
 h/cs?searchtype=author&amp\;query=Tang\,+S"><u>Sophia Tang</u></a>\, <a hre
 f="https://arxiv.org/search/cs?searchtype=author&amp\;query=Zhang\,+Y">Yinu
 o Zhang</a>\, <a href="https://arxiv.org/search/cs?searchtype=author&amp\;q
 uery=Tong\,+A">Alexander Tong</a>\, <a href="https://arxiv.org/search/cs?se
 archtype=author&amp\;query=Chatterjee\,+P">Pranam Chatterjee</a>)<br>Predic
 ting the intermediate trajectories between an initial and target distributi
 on is a central problem in generative modeling. Existing approaches\, such 
 as flow matching and Schrödinger Bridge Matching\, effectively learn mappin
 gs between two distributions by modeling a single stochastic path. However\
 , these methods are inherently limited to unimodal transitions and cannot c
 apture branched or divergent evolution from a common origin to multiple dis
 tinct outcomes. To address this\, we introduce Branched Schrödinger Bridge 
 Matching (BranchSBM)\, a novel framework that learns branched Schrödinger b
 ridges. BranchSBM parameterizes multiple time-dependent velocity fields and
  growth processes\, enabling the representation of population-level diverge
 nce into multiple terminal distributions. We show that BranchSBM is not onl
 y more expressive but also essential for tasks involving multi-path surface
  navigation\, modeling cell fate bifurcations from homogeneous progenitor s
 tates\, and simulating diverging cellular responses to perturbations.<br><b
 ><br></b><b>Meeting Details:<br></b><i>Every Monday at 9</i><i>:00 PT / 12:
 00 ET /  18:00 CE(S)T  </i><br><a href="https://zoom.us/j/5775722530?pwd=Zz
 lGTXlDNThhUDZOdU4vN2JRMm5pQT09" target="_blank">https://zoom.us/j/577572253
 0?pwd=ZzlGTXlDNThhUDZOdU4vN2JRMm5pQT09</a><br><br><b>Slack Workspace for di
