ORGANIZER;CN=vdujn2pbpkhgr5f55cm39qbmu8@group.calendar.google.com:mailto:vd
 ujn2pbpkhgr5f55cm39qbmu8@group.calendar.google.com
UID:b1qril1ke5kft3h5gbg6tqm1ob@google.com
ATTENDEE;CUTYPE=INDIVIDUAL;ROLE=REQ-PARTICIPANT;PARTSTAT=NEEDS-ACTION;CN=ro
 b.brekelmans@vectorinstitute.ai;X-NUM-GUESTS=0:mailto:rob.brekelmans@vector
 institute.ai
ATTENDEE;CUTYPE=INDIVIDUAL;ROLE=REQ-PARTICIPANT;PARTSTAT=ACCEPTED;CN=stephe
 nzhao@cs.toronto.edu;X-NUM-GUESTS=0:mailto:stephenzhao@cs.toronto.edu
RECURRENCE-ID;TZID=Europe/Berlin:20240916T180000
CREATED:20210724T162031Z
DESCRIPTION:<u></u><u></u><u></u><u></u><u></u><b>Speaker:<br></b>Rob Breke
 lmans who is a Postdoctoral Fellow at the Vector Institute in Toronto\, wor
 king with Alireza Makhzani and Roger Grosse. He graduated with my PhD from 
 University of Southern California in 2022\, working with Greg Ver Steeg and
  Aram Galstyan. He also interned with the AI Safety Analysis team at DeepMi
 nd <a href="https://deepmindsafetyresearch.medium.com/your-policy-regularis
 er-is-secretly-an-adversary-14684c743d45">(Blog)</a>\, working with Pedro O
 rtega and Tim Genewein<br><br><b>Paper:</b><br><b>Probabilistic Inference i
 n Language Models via Twisted Sequential Monte Carlo </b><a href="https://a
 rxiv.org/abs/2404.17546">https://arxiv.org/abs/2404.17546</a> (<a href="htt
 ps://arxiv.org/search/cs?searchtype=author&amp\;query=Zhao\,+S"><u>Stephen 
 Zhao</u></a>\, <a href="https://arxiv.org/search/cs?searchtype=author&amp\;
 query=Brekelmans\,+R">Rob Brekelmans</a>\, <a href="https://arxiv.org/searc
 h/cs?searchtype=author&amp\;query=Makhzani\,+A">Alireza Makhzani</a>\, <a h
 ref="https://arxiv.org/search/cs?searchtype=author&amp\;query=Grosse\,+R">R
 oger Grosse</a>)<br>Numerous capability and safety techniques of Large Lang
 uage Models (LLMs)\, including RLHF\, automated red-teaming\, prompt engine
 ering\, and infilling\, can be cast as sampling from an unnormalized target
  distribution defined by a given reward or potential function over the full
  sequence. In this work\, we leverage the rich toolkit of Sequential Monte 
 Carlo (SMC) for these probabilistic inference problems. In particular\, we 
 use learned twist functions to estimate the expected future value of the po
 tential at each timestep\, which enables us to focus inference-time computa
 tion on promising partial sequences. We propose a novel contrastive method 
 for learning the twist functions\, and establish connections with the rich 
 literature of soft reinforcement learning. As a complementary application o
 f our twisted SMC framework\, we present methods for evaluating the accurac
 y of language model inference techniques using novel bidirectional SMC boun
 ds on the log partition function. These bounds can be used to estimate the 
 KL divergence between the inference and target distributions in both direct
 ions. We apply our inference evaluation techniques to show that twisted SMC
  is effective for sampling undesirable outputs from a pretrained model (a u
 seful component of harmlessness training and automated red-teaming)\, gener
 ating reviews with varied sentiment\, and performing infilling tasks.<br><b
 ><br></b><b>Meeting Details:<br></b><i>Every Monday at 9</i><i>:00 PT / 12:
 00 ET /  18:00 CE(S)T  </i><br><a href="https://zoom.us/j/5775722530?pwd=Zz
 lGTXlDNThhUDZOdU4vN2JRMm5pQT09" target="_blank">https://zoom.us/j/577572253
 0?pwd=ZzlGTXlDNThhUDZOdU4vN2JRMm5pQT09</a><br><br><b>Slack Workspace for di
 scussion and paper voting:<br></b><a href="https://join.slack.com/t/logag/s
 hared_invite/zt-2nmy5vsj7-8KEDXe~mU~p5fCxwupSv5w" target="_blank">https://j
 oin.slack.com/t/logag/shared_invite/zt-2nmy5vsj7-8KEDXe~mU~p5fCxwupSv5w</a>
 <br><br><b>All information: Schedule of upcoming papers\, recordings\, mail
 ing list</b><b>:<br></b><a href="https://portal.valencelabs.com/logg" targe
 t="_blank">https://portal.valencelabs.com/logg</a><b><br></b><u></u><u></u>
 <u></u><u></u><u></u>
LAST-MODIFIED:20260320T170220Z
