ORGANIZER;CN=vdujn2pbpkhgr5f55cm39qbmu8@group.calendar.google.com:mailto:vd
 ujn2pbpkhgr5f55cm39qbmu8@group.calendar.google.com
UID:4om8dkr7eu2gvvk4o4mi4c9bjg_R20230327T150000@google.com
ATTENDEE;CUTYPE=INDIVIDUAL;ROLE=REQ-PARTICIPANT;PARTSTAT=NEEDS-ACTION;CN=br
 andstetter@ml.jku.at;X-NUM-GUESTS=0:mailto:brandstetter@ml.jku.at
ATTENDEE;CUTYPE=INDIVIDUAL;ROLE=REQ-PARTICIPANT;PARTSTAT=ACCEPTED;CN=david.
 ruhe@gmail.com;X-NUM-GUESTS=0:mailto:david.ruhe@gmail.com
RECURRENCE-ID;TZID=Europe/Berlin:20230703T170000
CREATED:20210724T162031Z
DESCRIPTION:<u></u><u></u><u></u><u></u><u></u><b>Speaker:<br></b>David Ruh
 e is a second-year Ph.D. student in the <a href="https://amlab.science.uva.
 nl/">Amsterdam Machine Learning Lab (AMLab)</a> and the <a href="https://ai
 4science-amsterdam.github.io/">AI4Science<sup></sup></a> lab. He works on d
 eveloping new machine learning algorithms that can help solve scientific re
 search questions. His current interests are scientific machine learning\, p
 hysics-inspired machine learning\, inverse problems\, generative models and
  self-supervised inference.<br><br><b>Paper:</b><br><b>Clifford Group Equiv
 ariant Neural Networks </b><a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2305.11141">https
 ://arxiv.org/abs/2305.11141</a> (<a href="https://arxiv.org/search/cs?searc
 htype=author&amp\;query=Ruhe%2C+D"><u>David Ruhe</u></a>\, <a href="https:/
 /arxiv.org/search/cs?searchtype=author&amp\;query=Brandstetter%2C+J">Johann
 es Brandstetter</a>\, <a href="https://arxiv.org/search/cs?searchtype=autho
 r&amp\;query=Forr%C3%A9%2C+P">Patrick Forré</a>)<br>We introduce Clifford G
 roup Equivariant Neural Networks: a novel approach for constructing E(n)-eq
 uivariant networks. We identify and study the Clifford group\, a subgroup i
 nside the Clifford algebra\, whose definition we slightly adjust to achieve
  several favorable properties. Primarily\, the group's action forms an orth
 ogonal automorphism that extends beyond the typical vector space to the ent
 ire Clifford algebra while respecting the multivector grading. This leads t
 o several non-equivalent subrepresentations corresponding to the multivecto
 r decomposition. Furthermore\, we prove that the action respects not just t
 he vector space structure of the Clifford algebra but also its multiplicati
 ve structure\, i.e.\, the geometric product. These findings imply that ever
 y polynomial in multivectors\, including their grade projections\, constitu
 tes an equivariant map with respect to the Clifford group\, allowing us to 
 parameterize equivariant neural network layers. Notable advantages are that
  these layers operate directly on a vector basis and elegantly generalize t
 o any dimension. We demonstrate\, notably from a single core implementation
 \, state-of-the-art performance on several distinct tasks\, including a thr
 ee-dimensional n-body experiment\, a four-dimensional Lorentz-equivariant h
 igh-energy physics experiment\, and a five-dimensional convex hull experime
 nt.<br><b><br></b><b>Meeting Details:<br></b><i>Every Monday at</i><i> </i>
 <i>5pm CEST / 3pm UTC / 11am EDT / 8am PDT.  </i><br><a href="https://zoom.
 us/j/5775722530?pwd=ZzlGTXlDNThhUDZOdU4vN2JRMm5pQT09">https://zoom.us/j/577
 5722530?pwd=ZzlGTXlDNThhUDZOdU4vN2JRMm5pQT09</a><br><br><b>Slack Workspace 
 for discussion and paper voting:<br></b><a href="https://join.slack.com/t/l
 ogag/shared_invite/zt-1hcqybjyd-ty5Pbj~BQy~W4B4mQQVxDQ">https://join.slack.
 com/t/logag/shared_invite/zt-1hcqybjyd-ty5Pbj~BQy~W4B4mQQVxDQ</a><br><br><b
 >All information: Schedule of upcoming papers\, recordings\, mailing list</
 b><b>:<br></b><u><a href="https://hannes-stark.com/logag-reading-group">htt
 ps://hannes-stark.com/logag-reading-group</a></u><b><br></b><u></u><u></u><
 u></u><u></u><u></u>
LAST-MODIFIED:20250330T035016Z
