BEGIN:VEVENT
DTSTART;TZID=Europe/Berlin:20240422T170000
DTEND;TZID=Europe/Berlin:20240422T180000
DTSTAMP:20260325T005103Z
ORGANIZER;CN=vdujn2pbpkhgr5f55cm39qbmu8@group.calendar.google.com:mailto:vd
 ujn2pbpkhgr5f55cm39qbmu8@group.calendar.google.com
UID:4om8dkr7eu2gvvk4o4mi4c9bjg_R20231211T160000@google.com
ATTENDEE;CUTYPE=INDIVIDUAL;ROLE=REQ-PARTICIPANT;PARTSTAT=NEEDS-ACTION;CN=se
 rgey.pozdnyakov@epfl.ch;X-NUM-GUESTS=0:mailto:sergey.pozdnyakov@epfl.ch
ATTENDEE;CUTYPE=INDIVIDUAL;ROLE=REQ-PARTICIPANT;PARTSTAT=ACCEPTED;CN=vdujn2
 pbpkhgr5f55cm39qbmu8@group.calendar.google.com;X-NUM-GUESTS=0:mailto:vdujn2
 pbpkhgr5f55cm39qbmu8@group.calendar.google.com
RECURRENCE-ID;TZID=Europe/Berlin:20240422T170000
CREATED:20210724T162031Z
DESCRIPTION:<u></u><u></u><u></u><u></u><u></u><b>Speaker:<br></b>Sergey Po
 zdnyakov is a final year PhD student at EPFL <a href="https://scholar.googl
 e.com/citations?hl=en&amp\;user=1-uZ3uYAAAAJ&amp\;view_op=list_works">worki
 ng on</a> the theory and practical developments of machine learning interat
 omic potentials.<br><br><b>Paper:</b><br><b>Smooth\, exact rotational symme
 trization for deep learning on point clouds </b><a href="https://arxiv.org/
 abs/2305.19302">https://arxiv.org/abs/2305.19302</a> (<a href="https://arxi
 v.org/search/cs?searchtype=author&amp\;query=Pozdnyakov\,+S+N">Sergey N. Po
 zdnyakov</a>\, <a href="https://arxiv.org/search/cs?searchtype=author&amp\;
 query=Ceriotti\,+M">Michele Ceriotti</a>)<br>Point clouds are versatile rep
 resentations of 3D objects and have found widespread application in science
  and engineering. Many successful deep-learning models have been proposed t
 hat use them as input. The domain of chemical and materials modeling is esp
 ecially challenging because exact compliance with physical constraints is h
 ighly desirable for a model to be usable in practice. These constraints inc
 lude smoothness and invariance with respect to translations\, rotations\, a
 nd permutations of identical atoms. If these requirements are not rigorousl
 y fulfilled\, atomistic simulations might lead to absurd outcomes even if t
 he model has excellent accuracy. Consequently\, dedicated architectures\, w
 hich achieve invariance by restricting their design space\, have been devel
 oped. General-purpose point-cloud models are more varied but often disregar
 d rotational symmetry. We propose a general symmetrization method that adds
  rotational equivariance to any given model while preserving all the other 
 requirements. Our approach simplifies the development of better atomic-scal
 e machine-learning schemes by relaxing the constraints on the design space 
 and making it possible to incorporate ideas that proved effective in other 
 domains. We demonstrate this idea by introducing the Point Edge Transformer
  (PET) architecture\, which is not intrinsically equivariant but achieves s
 tate-of-the-art performance on several benchmark datasets of molecules and 
 solids. A-posteriori application of our general protocol makes PET exactly 
 equivariant\, with minimal changes to its accuracy.<br><b><br></b><b>Meetin
 g Details:<br></b><i>Every Monday at</i><i> </i><i>5pm CST / 4pm UTC / 11am
  EST / 8am PST.  </i><br><a href="https://zoom.us/j/5775722530?pwd=ZzlGTXlD
 NThhUDZOdU4vN2JRMm5pQT09">https://zoom.us/j/5775722530?pwd=ZzlGTXlDNThhUDZO
 dU4vN2JRMm5pQT09</a><br><br><b>Slack Workspace for discussion and paper vot
 ing:<br></b><a href="https://join.slack.com/t/logag/shared_invite/zt-22y7n3
 k7a-FHwX31gc85yZCa0uF8BU7w">https://join.slack.com/t/logag/shared_invite/zt
 -22y7n3k7a-FHwX31gc85yZCa0uF8BU7w</a><br><br><b>All information: Schedule o
 f upcoming papers\, recordings\, mailing list</b><b>:<br></b><u><a href="ht
 tps://hannes-stark.com/logag-reading-group">https://hannes-stark.com/logag-
 reading-group</a></u><b><br></b><u></u><u></u><u></u><u></u><u></u>
LAST-MODIFIED:20240904T130202Z
LOCATION:https://zoom.us/j/5775722530?pwd=ZzlGTXlDNThhUDZOdU4vN2JRMm5pQT09
SEQUENCE:1
STATUS:CONFIRMED
SUMMARY:Learning on Graphs and Geometry Reading Group
TRANSP:OPAQUE
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
DTSTART;TZID=Europe/Berlin:20240701T170000
DTEND;TZID=Europe/Berlin:20240701T180000
DTSTAMP:20260325T005103Z
ORGANIZER;CN=vdujn2pbpkhgr5f55cm39qbmu8@group.calendar.google.com:mailto:vd
 ujn2pbpkhgr5f55cm39qbmu8@group.calendar.google.com
UID:4om8dkr7eu2gvvk4o4mi4c9bjg_R20231211T160000@google.com
ATTENDEE;CUTYPE=INDIVIDUAL;ROLE=REQ-PARTICIPANT;PARTSTAT=NEEDS-ACTION;CN=ka
 cper.kapusniak@keble.ox.ac.uk;X-NUM-GUESTS=0:mailto:kacper.kapusniak@keble.
 ox.ac.uk
RECURRENCE-ID;TZID=Europe/Berlin:20240701T170000
CREATED:20210724T162031Z
DESCRIPTION:<u></u><u></u><u></u><u></u><u></u><b>Speaker:<br></b>Kacper Ka
 pusniak.<br><br><b>Paper:</b><br><b>Metric Flow Matching for Smooth Interpo
 lations on the Data Manifold </b><a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2405.14780"
 >https://arxiv.org/abs/2405.14780</a> (<a href="https://arxiv.org/search/cs
 ?searchtype=author&amp\;query=Kapusniak\,+K"><u>Kacper Kapusniak</u></a>\, 
 <a href="https://arxiv.org/search/cs?searchtype=author&amp\;query=Potaptchi
 k\,+P">Peter Potaptchik</a>\, <a href="https://arxiv.org/search/cs?searchty
 pe=author&amp\;query=Reu\,+T">Teodora Reu</a>\, <a href="https://arxiv.org/
 search/cs?searchtype=author&amp\;query=Zhang\,+L">Leo Zhang</a>\, <a href="
 https://arxiv.org/search/cs?searchtype=author&amp\;query=Tong\,+A">Alexande
 r Tong</a>\, <a href="https://arxiv.org/search/cs?searchtype=author&amp\;qu
 ery=Bronstein\,+M">Michael Bronstein</a>\, <a href="https://arxiv.org/searc
 h/cs?searchtype=author&amp\;query=Bose\,+A+J">Avishek Joey Bose</a>\, <a hr
 ef="https://arxiv.org/search/cs?searchtype=author&amp\;query=Di+Giovanni\,+
 F">Francesco Di Giovanni</a>)<br>Matching objectives underpin the success o
 f modern generative models and rely on constructing conditional paths that 
 transform a source distribution into a target distribution. Despite being a
  fundamental building block\, conditional paths have been designed principa
 lly under the assumption of Euclidean geometry\, resulting in straight inte
 rpolations. However\, this can be particularly restrictive for tasks such a
 s trajectory inference\, where straight paths might lie outside the data ma
 nifold\, thus failing to capture the underlying dynamics giving rise to the
  observed marginals. In this paper\, we propose Metric Flow Matching (MFM)\
 , a novel simulation-free framework for conditional flow matching where int
 erpolants are approximate geodesics learned by minimizing the kinetic energ
 y of a data-induced Riemannian metric. This way\, the generative model matc
 hes vector fields on the data manifold\, which corresponds to lower uncerta
 inty and more meaningful interpolations. We prescribe general metrics to in
 stantiate MFM\, independent of the task\, and test it on a suite of challen
 ging problems including LiDAR navigation\, unpaired image translation\, and
  modeling cellular dynamics. We observe that MFM outperforms the Euclidean 
 baselines\, particularly achieving SOTA on single-cell trajectory predictio
 n.<br><b><br></b><b>Meeting Details:<br></b><i>Every Monday at</i><i> </i><
 i>5pm CST / 4pm UTC / 11am EST / 8am PST.  </i><br><a href="https://zoom.us
