ATTENDEE;CUTYPE=INDIVIDUAL;ROLE=REQ-PARTICIPANT;PARTSTAT=ACCEPTED;CN=zc1216
 @nyu.edu;X-NUM-GUESTS=0:mailto:zc1216@nyu.edu
ATTENDEE;CUTYPE=INDIVIDUAL;ROLE=REQ-PARTICIPANT;PARTSTAT=NEEDS-ACTION;CN=sv
 illar3@jhu.edu;X-NUM-GUESTS=0:mailto:svillar3@jhu.edu
RECURRENCE-ID;TZID=Europe/Berlin:20211116T170000
CREATED:20210724T162031Z
DESCRIPTION:<b>Speakers:<br></b><i>Joint authors&nbsp\;of the paper&nbsp\;<
 a href="https://cims.nyu.edu/~chenzh/">Zhengdao Chen</a>&nbsp\;and Prof.&nb
 sp\;<a href="https://www.ams.jhu.edu/villar/">Soledad Villar</a>. Zhengdao 
 is a PhD student in Mathematics at New York University. Prof. Villar holds 
 her position&nbsp\;at the&nbsp\;<a href="https://engineering.jhu.edu/ams">D
 epartment of Applied Mathematics &amp\; Statistics</a>\, and&nbsp\;<a href=
 "https://www.minds.jhu.edu/">Mathematical Institute for Data Science</a>&nb
 sp\;at Johns Hopkins University. She is interested in ML\, optimization\, g
 raph representation learning\, and GNNs.</i><br><br><b>Paper:</b><br><b>On 
 the equivalence between graph isomorphism testing and function approximatio
 n with GNNs </b><a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/1905.12560">https://arxiv.or
 g/abs/1905.12560</a><b> (</b><a href="https://arxiv.org/search/cs?searchtyp
 e=author&query=Chen%2C+Z">Zhengdao Chen</a>\,&nbsp\;<a href="https://arxiv.
 org/search/cs?searchtype=author&query=Villar%2C+S">Soledad Villar</a>\,&nbs
 p\;<a href="https://arxiv.org/search/cs?searchtype=author&query=Chen%2C+L">
 Lei Chen</a>\,&nbsp\;<a href="https://arxiv.org/search/cs?searchtype=author
 &query=Bruna%2C+J">Joan Bruna</a>)<br>Graph neural networks (GNNs) have ach
 ieved lots of success on graph-structured data. In the light of this\, ther
 e has been increasing interest in studying their representation power. One 
 line of work focuses on the universal approximation of permutation-invarian
 t functions by certain classes of GNNs\, and another demonstrates the limit
 ation of GNNs via graph isomorphism tests.<br>Our work connects these two p
 erspectives and proves their equivalence. We further develop a framework of
  the representation power of GNNs with the language of sigma-algebra\, whic
 h incorporates both viewpoints. Using this framework\, we compare the expre
 ssive power of different classes of GNNs as well as other methods on graphs
 . In particular\, we prove that order-2 Graph G-invariant networks fail to 
 distinguish non-isomorphic regular graphs with the same degree. We then ext
 end them to a new architecture\, Ring-GNNs\, which succeeds on distinguishi
 ng these graphs and provides improvements on real-world social network data
 sets.<br><b><br></b><b>Meeting Details:<br></b><i>Every Tuesday at 5pm CET 
 / 4pm UTC / 11am EDT / 8am PT</i><br><a href="https://us02web.zoom.us/j/455
 6095405?pwd=UmZRbC95d050TkRsZ2QvWER3SDdqUT09">https://us02web.zoom.us/j/455
 6095405?pwd=UmZRbC95d050TkRsZ2QvWER3SDdqUT09</a><br><br><b>Slack Workspace 
 for discussion and paper voting:<br></b><a href="https://join.slack.com/t/l
 ogag/shared_invite/zt-u0mbo1ec-zElmvd1oSCXGjXvxLSokvg">https://join.slack.c
 om/t/<u></u>logag/shared_invite/zt-<u></u>u0mbo1ec-<u></u>zElmvd1oSCXGjXvxL
 Sokvg</a><br><br><b>All information: Schedule of upcoming papers\, recordin
 gs</b><b>:<br></b><a href="https://hannes-stark.com/logag-reading-group" id
 ="ow1531" __is_owner="true">https://hannes-stark.com/logag-reading-group</a
 ><b><br></b>
LAST-MODIFIED:20250330T034826Z
SEQUENCE:0
STATUS:CONFIRMED
SUMMARY:Learning on Graphs and Geometry Reading Group
TRANSP:OPAQUE
END:VEVENT
