 ckmann@ucsd.edu;X-NUM-GUESTS=0:mailto:peckmann@ucsd.edu
ATTENDEE;CUTYPE=INDIVIDUAL;ROLE=REQ-PARTICIPANT;PARTSTAT=TENTATIVE;CN=rosey
 u@ucsd.edu;X-NUM-GUESTS=0:mailto:roseyu@ucsd.edu
ATTENDEE;CUTYPE=INDIVIDUAL;ROLE=REQ-PARTICIPANT;PARTSTAT=ACCEPTED;CN=petere
 ckmann@gmail.com;X-NUM-GUESTS=0:mailto:petereckmann@gmail.com
RECURRENCE-ID;TZID=Europe/Berlin:20240325T170000
CREATED:20210724T162031Z
DESCRIPTION:<u></u><u></u><u></u><u></u><u></u><b>Speaker:<br></b><a href="
 https://petereckmann1.github.io/">Peter Eckmann </a>who is a third-year und
 ergraduate student at University of California San Diego pursuing a B.S. in
  Computer Science. He is a member of <a href="https://roseyu.com/"><u>Dr. R
 ose Yu’s</u></a> Spatiotemporal Machine Learning Lab. Professor Michael Gil
 son (co-author) will also join.<br><br><b>Paper:</b><br><b>MFBind: a Multi-
 Fidelity Approach for Evaluating Drug Compounds in Practical Generative Mod
 eling </b><a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2402.10387">https://arxiv.org/abs/
 2402.10387</a> (<a href="https://arxiv.org/search/q-bio?searchtype=author&a
 mp\;query=Eckmann\,+P">Peter Eckmann</a>\, <a href="https://arxiv.org/searc
 h/q-bio?searchtype=author&amp\;query=Wu\,+D">Dongxia Wu</a>\, <a href="http
 s://arxiv.org/search/q-bio?searchtype=author&amp\;query=Heinzelmann\,+G">Ge
 rmano Heinzelmann</a>\, <a href="https://arxiv.org/search/q-bio?searchtype=
 author&amp\;query=Gilson\,+M+K">Michael K Gilson</a>\, <a href="https://arx
 iv.org/search/q-bio?searchtype=author&amp\;query=Yu\,+R">Rose Yu</a>)<br>Cu
 rrent generative models for drug discovery primarily use molecular docking 
 to evaluate the quality of generated compounds. However\, such models are o
 ften not useful in practice because even compounds with high docking scores
  do not consistently show experimental activity. More accurate methods for 
 activity prediction exist\, such as molecular dynamics based binding free e
 nergy calculations\, but they are too computationally expensive to use in a
  generative model. We propose a multi-fidelity approach\, Multi-Fidelity Bi
 nd (MFBind)\, to achieve the optimal trade-off between accuracy and computa
 tional cost. MFBind integrates docking and binding free energy simulators t
 o train a multi-fidelity deep surrogate model with active learning. Our dee
 p surrogate model utilizes a pretraining technique and linear prediction he
 ads to efficiently fit small amounts of high-fidelity data. We perform exte
 nsive experiments and show that MFBind (1) outperforms other state-of-the-a
 rt single and multi-fidelity baselines in surrogate modeling\, and (2) boos
 ts the performance of generative models with markedly higher quality compou
 nds.<br><b><br></b><b>Meeting Details:<br></b><i>Every Monday at</i><i> </i
 ><i>5pm CST / 4pm UTC / 11am EST / 8am PST.  </i><br><a href="https://zoom.
 us/j/5775722530?pwd=ZzlGTXlDNThhUDZOdU4vN2JRMm5pQT09">https://zoom.us/j/577
 5722530?pwd=ZzlGTXlDNThhUDZOdU4vN2JRMm5pQT09</a><br><br><b>Slack Workspace 
 for discussion and paper voting:<br></b><a href="https://join.slack.com/t/l
 ogag/shared_invite/zt-22y7n3k7a-FHwX31gc85yZCa0uF8BU7w">https://join.slack.
 com/t/logag/shared_invite/zt-22y7n3k7a-FHwX31gc85yZCa0uF8BU7w</a><br><br><b
 >All information: Schedule of upcoming papers\, recordings\, mailing list</
 b><b>:<br></b><u><a href="https://hannes-stark.com/logag-reading-group">htt
 ps://hannes-stark.com/logag-reading-group</a></u><b><br></b><u></u><u></u><
 u></u><u></u><u></u>
