 enyang.huang@mail.mcgill.ca;X-NUM-GUESTS=0:mailto:shenyang.huang@mail.mcgil
 l.ca
RECURRENCE-ID;TZID=Europe/Berlin:20221107T170000
CREATED:20210724T162031Z
DESCRIPTION:<html-blob><u></u><u></u><u></u><u></u><b>Speaker:<br></b>The a
 uthors Farimah Poursafaei and Andy Huang! Andy Huang is a PhD student at Mi
 la and McGill university.<br><br><b>Paper:</b><br><b>Towards Better Evaluat
 ion for Dynamic Link Prediction&nbsp\;</b></html-blob><a href="https://open
 review.net/forum?id=1GVpwr2Tfdg" style="background-color: var(--textfield-s
 urface)\;">https://openreview.net/forum?id=1GVpwr2Tfdg</a><b style="backgro
 und-color: var(--textfield-surface)\; color: var(--on-surface)\;">&nbsp\;(<
 /b><span style="background-color: var(--textfield-surface)\; color: var(--o
 n-surface)\;">Farimah Poursafaei\, Andy Huang\, Kellin Pelrine\, Reihaneh R
 abbany)</span><br><html-blob>Despite the prevalence of recent success in le
 arning from static graphs\, learning from time-evolving graphs remains an o
 pen challenge. In this work\, we design new\, more stringent evaluation pro
 cedures for link prediction specific to dynamic graphs\, which reflect real
 -world considerations\, to better compare the strengths and weaknesses of m
 ethods. First\, we create two visualization techniques to understand the re
 occurring patterns of edges over time and show that many edges reoccur at l
 ater time steps. Based on this observation\, we propose a pure memorization
  baseline called EdgeBank. EdgeBank achieves surprisingly strong performanc
 e across multiple settings because easy negative edges are often used in th
 e current evaluation setting. To evaluate against more difficult negative e
 dges\, we introduce two more challenging negative sampling strategies that 
 improve robustness and better match real-world applications. Lastly\, we in
 troduce six new dynamic graph datasets from a diverse set of domains missin
 g from current benchmarks\, providing new challenges and opportunities for 
 future research. Our code repository is accessible at <a href="https://gith
 ub.com/fpour/DGB.git">https://github.com/fpour/DGB.git</a>.<br><br><b><br><
 /b><b>Meeting Details:<br></b><i>Every Monday at</i><i> </i><i>5pm CEST / 3
 pm UTC / 11am EDT / 8am PDT.&nbsp\;&nbsp\;</i><br><a href="https://zoom.us/
 j/5775722530?pwd=ZzlGTXlDNThhUDZOdU4vN2JRMm5pQT09">https://zoom.us/j/577572
 2530?pwd=ZzlGTXlDNThhUDZOdU4vN2JRMm5pQT09</a><br><br><b>Slack Workspace for
  discussion and paper voting:<br></b><a href="https://join.slack.com/t/loga
 g/shared_invite/zt-1hcqybjyd-ty5Pbj~BQy~W4B4mQQVxDQ">https://join.slack.com
 /t/logag/shared_invite/zt-1hcqybjyd-ty5Pbj~BQy~W4B4mQQVxDQ</a><br><br><b>Al
 l information: Schedule of upcoming papers\, recordings\, mailing list</b><
 b>:<br></b><u><a href="https://hannes-stark.com/logag-reading-group">https:
 //hannes-stark.com/logag-reading-group</a></u><b><br></b><u></u><u></u><u><
 /u><u></u></html-blob>
