BEGIN:VEVENT
DTSTART;TZID=Europe/Berlin:20211123T170000
DTEND;TZID=Europe/Berlin:20211123T180000
DTSTAMP:20260325T005103Z
UID:1hfd2s2r4hmta9og6idelbrfic_R20211116T160000@google.com
RECURRENCE-ID;TZID=Europe/Berlin:20211123T170000
CREATED:20210724T162031Z
DESCRIPTION:<b>Speaker:<br></b>First author&nbsp\;<a href="https://dongkwan
 -kim.github.io/">Dongkwan Kim</a>&nbsp\;who is a Ph.D. student at KAIST Sch
 ool of Computing\, advised by&nbsp\;<a href="https://aliceoh9.github.io/">A
 lice Oh</a>. His research interest is graph representation learning models 
 and applications to social networks\, code\, or molecules.<br><br><b>Paper:
 </b><br><b>How to Find Your Friendly Neighborhood: Graph Attention Design w
 ith Self-Supervision</b>&nbsp\;<a href="https://openreview.net/forum?id=Wi5
 KUNlqWty">https://openreview.net/forum?id=Wi5KUNlqWty</a>&nbsp\;(<i><a href
 ="https://openreview.net/profile?id=~Dongkwan_Kim1">Dongkwan Kim</a></i><i>
 \,&nbsp\;</i><i><a href="https://openreview.net/profile?id=~Alice_Oh1">Alic
 e Oh</a></i>)<br>Attention mechanism in graph neural networks is designed t
 o assign larger weights to important neighbor nodes for better representati
 on. However\, what graph attention learns is not understood well\, particul
 arly when graphs are noisy. In this paper\, we propose a self-supervised gr
 aph attention network (SuperGAT)\, an improved graph attention model for no
 isy graphs. Specifically\, we exploit two attention forms compatible with a
  self-supervised task to predict edges\, whose presence and absence contain
  the inherent information about the importance of the relationships between
  nodes. By encoding edges\, SuperGAT learns more expressive attention in di
 stinguishing mislinked neighbors. We find two graph characteristics influen
 ce the effectiveness of attention forms and self-supervision: homophily and
  average degree. Thus\, our recipe provides guidance on which attention des
 ign to use when those two graph characteristics are known. Our experiment o
 n 17 real-world datasets demonstrates that our recipe generalizes across 15
  datasets of them\, and our models designed by recipe show improved perform
 ance over baselines.<br><b><br></b><b>Meeting Details:<br></b><i>Every Tues
 day at 5pm CET / 4pm UTC / 11am EDT / 8am PT</i><br><a href="https://us02we
 b.zoom.us/j/4556095405?pwd=UmZRbC95d050TkRsZ2QvWER3SDdqUT09">https://us02we
 b.zoom.us/j/4556095405?pwd=UmZRbC95d050TkRsZ2QvWER3SDdqUT09</a><br><br><b>S
 lack Workspace for discussion and paper voting:<br></b><a href="https://joi
 n.slack.com/t/logag/shared_invite/zt-u0mbo1ec-zElmvd1oSCXGjXvxLSokvg">https
 ://join.slack.com/t/<u></u>logag/shared_invite/zt-<u></u>u0mbo1ec-<u></u>zE
 lmvd1oSCXGjXvxLSokvg</a><br><br><b>All information: Schedule of upcoming pa
 pers\, recordings\, mailing list</b><b>:<br></b><a href="https://hannes-sta
 rk.com/logag-reading-group" id="ow1752" __is_owner="true">https://hannes-st
 ark.com/logag-reading-group</a><b><br></b>
LAST-MODIFIED:20220820T195612Z
SEQUENCE:0
STATUS:CONFIRMED
SUMMARY:Learning on Graphs and Geometry Reading Group
TRANSP:OPAQUE
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
DTSTART;TZID=Europe/Berlin:20210907T170000
DTEND;TZID=Europe/Berlin:20210907T180000
DTSTAMP:20260325T005103Z
UID:1hfd2s2r4hmta9og6idelbrfic_R20210824T150000@google.com
RECURRENCE-ID;TZID=Europe/Berlin:20210907T170000
CREATED:20210724T162031Z
DESCRIPTION:<b>Speaker:<br></b>Joint first authors of the paper <a href="ht
 tps://crisbodnar.github.io/" id="ow2222" __is_owner="true">Cristian Bodnar<
 /a> and <a href="https://scholar.google.com/citations?user=PT2CDA4AAAAJ&hl=
 en" id="ow2226" __is_owner="true">Fabrizio Frasca</a>. Cristian is a&nbsp\;
 second-year PhD student at Cambridge supervised by&nbsp\;<a href="https://w
 ww.cl.cam.ac.uk/~pl219/" id="ow2230" __is_owner="true">Prof Pietro Liò</a>.
  He works on topological and geometric deep learning. Fabrizio&nbsp\;is a P
 hD candidate at Imperial College London supervised by Prof. <a href="https:
 //www.imperial.ac.uk/people/m.bronstein" id="ow2234" __is_owner="true">Mich
 ael Bronstein</a>&nbsp\;and he works as an ML researcher at Twitter.<br><br
 ><b>Paper:</b><br><b>Weisfeiler and Lehman Go Cellular: CW Networks</b>&nbs
 p\;<a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2106.12575">https://arxiv.org/abs/2106.12
 575</a>&nbsp\;(<a href="https://arxiv.org/search/cs?searchtype=author&query
 =Bodnar%2C+C">Cristian Bodnar</a>\,&nbsp\;<a href="https://arxiv.org/search
 /cs?searchtype=author&query=Frasca%2C+F">Fabrizio Frasca</a>\,&nbsp\;<a hre
 f="https://arxiv.org/search/cs?searchtype=author&query=Otter%2C+N">Nina Ott
 er</a>\,&nbsp\;<a href="https://arxiv.org/search/cs?searchtype=author&query
 =Wang%2C+Y+G">Yu Guang Wang</a>\,&nbsp\;<a href="https://arxiv.org/search/c
 s?searchtype=author&query=Li%C3%B2%2C+P">Pietro Liò</a>\,&nbsp\;<a href="ht
 tps://arxiv.org/search/cs?searchtype=author&query=Mont%C3%BAfar%2C+G">Guido
  Montúfar</a>\,&nbsp\;<a href="https://arxiv.org/search/cs?searchtype=autho
 r&query=Bronstein%2C+M">Michael Bronstein</a>)<br>Graph Neural Networks (GN
 Ns) are limited in their expressive power\, struggle with long-range intera
 ctions and lack a principled way to model higher-order structures. These pr
 oblems can be attributed to the strong coupling between the computational g
 raph and the input graph structure. The recently proposed Message Passing S
 implicial Networks naturally decouple these elements by performing message 
 passing on the clique complex of the graph. Nevertheless\, these models are
  severely constrained by the rigid combinatorial structure of Simplicial Co
 mplexes (SCs). In this work\, we extend recent theoretical results on SCs t
 o regular Cell Complexes\, topological objects that flexibly subsume SCs an
 d graphs. We show that this generalisation provides a powerful set of graph
  ``lifting'' transformations\, each leading to a unique hierarchical messag
 e passing procedure. The resulting methods\, which we collectively call CW 
 Networks (CWNs)\, are strictly more powerful than the WL test and\, in cert
 ain cases\, not less powerful than the 3-WL test. In particular\, we demons
 trate the effectiveness of one such scheme\, based on rings\, when applied 
 to molecular graph problems. The proposed architecture benefits from provab
 ly larger expressivity than commonly used GNNs\, principled modelling of hi
 gher-order signals and from compressing the distances between nodes. We dem
 onstrate that our model achieves state-of-the-art results on a variety of m
 olecular datasets.<br><b><br></b><b>Meeting Details:<br></b><i>Every Tuesda
 y at 5pm CEST / 3pm UTC / 11am EST / 8am PST</i><br><a href="https://us02we
 b.zoom.us/j/4556095405?pwd=UmZRbC95d050TkRsZ2QvWER3SDdqUT09">https://us02we
 b.zoom.us/j/4556095405?pwd=UmZRbC95d050TkRsZ2QvWER3SDdqUT09</a><br>Meeting 
 ID: 455 609 5405<br>Passcode: logag<br><br><b>Join Slack Workspace for disc
 ussions beyond the meetings and paper voting:<br></b><a href="https://join.
 slack.com/t/logag/shared_invite/zt-u0mbo1ec-zElmvd1oSCXGjXvxLSokvg">https:/
 /join.slack.com/t/<u></u>logag/shared_invite/zt-<u></u>u0mbo1ec-<u></u>zElm
 vd1oSCXGjXvxLSokvg</a><br><br><b>All information: Schedule of upcoming talk
 s\, slides + notes of previously covered papers</b><b>:<br></b><a href="htt
 p:///">https://hannes-stark.com/logag-reading-group</a><b><br></b>
LAST-MODIFIED:20220820T195612Z
SEQUENCE:0
STATUS:CONFIRMED
SUMMARY:Learning on Graphs and Geometry Reading Group
TRANSP:OPAQUE
END:VEVENT
