BEGIN:VEVENT
DTSTART;TZID=Europe/Berlin:20220531T170000
DTEND;TZID=Europe/Berlin:20220531T180000
DTSTAMP:20260325T005103Z
ORGANIZER;CN=vdujn2pbpkhgr5f55cm39qbmu8@group.calendar.google.com:mailto:vd
 ujn2pbpkhgr5f55cm39qbmu8@group.calendar.google.com
UID:FEEAEC38-0494-443E-BE84-F231E30E5AF5
ATTENDEE;CUTYPE=INDIVIDUAL;ROLE=REQ-PARTICIPANT;PARTSTAT=NEEDS-ACTION;CN=ba
 stian@rieck.me;X-NUM-GUESTS=0:mailto:bastian@rieck.me
ATTENDEE;CUTYPE=INDIVIDUAL;ROLE=REQ-PARTICIPANT;PARTSTAT=ACCEPTED;CN=edward
 .debrouwer@gmail.com;X-NUM-GUESTS=0:mailto:edward.debrouwer@gmail.com
RECURRENCE-ID;TZID=Europe/Berlin:20220531T170000
CREATED:20220820T195616Z
DESCRIPTION:<span><u></u><u></u><b>Speaker:<br /></b><a href="https://schol
 ar.google.com/citations?user=-Pm4XtAAAAAJ&amp\;hl">Edward De Brouwer </a>is
  a last year PhD student at KU Leuven\, Belgium on machine learning for hea
 lthcare. His research focuses on developing robust and trustworthy methods 
 for the clinical domain\, using neural differential equations\, topological
  data analysis and causal inference\, among others. In 2020-2021\, he was a
  visiting researcher at ETH Zürich\, where he worked on topological methods
  for graph neural networks. <br /><br /><b>Paper:</b><br /><b>Topological G
 raph Neural Networks</b> <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2102.07835">https:/
 /arxiv.org/abs/2102.07835</a> (<a href="https://arxiv.org/search/cs?searcht
 ype=author&amp\;query=Horn%2C+M">Max Horn</a>\, <a href="https://arxiv.org/
 search/cs?searchtype=author&amp\;query=De+Brouwer%2C+E">Edward De Brouwer</
 a>\, <a href="https://arxiv.org/search/cs?searchtype=author&amp\;query=Moor
 %2C+M">Michael Moor</a>\, <a href="https://arxiv.org/search/cs?searchtype=a
 uthor&amp\;query=Moreau%2C+Y">Yves Moreau</a>\, <a href="https://arxiv.org/
 search/cs?searchtype=author&amp\;query=Rieck%2C+B">Bastian Rieck</a>\, <a h
 ref="https://arxiv.org/search/cs?searchtype=author&amp\;query=Borgwardt%2C+
 K">Karsten Borgwardt</a>) ICLR 2022<br />Graph neural networks (GNNs) are a
  powerful architecture for tackling graph learning tasks\, yet have been sh
 own to be oblivious to eminent substructures such as cycles. We present TOG
 L\, a novel layer that incorporates global topological information of a gra
 ph using persistent homology. TOGL can be easily integrated into any type o
 f GNN and is strictly more expressive (in terms the Weisfeiler--Lehman grap
 h isomorphism test) than message-passing GNNs. Augmenting GNNs with TOGL le
 ads to improved predictive performance for graph and node classification ta
 sks\, both on synthetic data sets\, which can be classified by humans using
  their topology but not by ordinary GNNs\, and on real-world data.<b><br />
 <br /></b><b>Meeting Details:<br /></b><i>Every Tuesday at</i><i> </i><i>5p
 m CEST / 3pm UTC / 11am EST / 8am PDT.  </i><br /><a href="https://zoom.us/
 j/5775722530?pwd=ZzlGTXlDNThhUDZOdU4vN2JRMm5pQT09">https://zoom.us/j/577572
 2530?pwd=ZzlGTXlDNThhUDZOdU4vN2JRMm5pQT09</a><br /><br /><b>Slack Workspace
  for discussion and paper voting:<br /></b><a href="https://join.slack.com/
 t/logag/shared_invite/zt-u0mbo1ec-zElmvd1oSCXGjXvxLSokvg">https://join.slac
 k.com/t/<u></u>logag/shared_invite/zt-<u></u>u0mbo1ec-<u></u>zElmvd1oSCXGjX
 vxLSokvg</a><br /><br /><b>All information: Schedule of upcoming papers\, r
 ecordings\, mailing list</b><b>:<br /></b><u><a href="https://hannes-stark.
 com/logag-reading-group">https://hannes-stark.com/logag-reading-group</a></
 u><b><br /></b><u></u><u></u></span>
LAST-MODIFIED:20250330T034846Z
