BEGIN:VEVENT
DTSTART;TZID=Europe/Berlin:20211012T170000
DTEND;TZID=Europe/Berlin:20211012T180000
DTSTAMP:20260325T005103Z
ORGANIZER;CN=vdujn2pbpkhgr5f55cm39qbmu8@group.calendar.google.com:mailto:vd
 ujn2pbpkhgr5f55cm39qbmu8@group.calendar.google.com
UID:1hfd2s2r4hmta9og6idelbrfic_R20210824T150000@google.com
ATTENDEE;CUTYPE=INDIVIDUAL;ROLE=REQ-PARTICIPANT;PARTSTAT=ACCEPTED;CN=haiyan
 g@tamu.edu;X-NUM-GUESTS=0:mailto:haiyang@tamu.edu
RECURRENCE-ID;TZID=Europe/Berlin:20211012T170000
CREATED:20210724T162031Z
DESCRIPTION:<b>Speaker:<br></b>Author of the paper&nbsp\;<a href="https://s
 cholar.google.com/citations?user=LZKU1hUAAAAJ&amp\;hl=en">Haiyang Yu</a>&nb
 sp\;who is a PhD student at Texas A&amp\;M University supervised by Prof.&n
 bsp\;<a href="http://people.tamu.edu/~sji/">Shuiwang Ji</a>.<br><br><b>Pape
 r:</b><br><b>On Explainability of Graph Neural Networks via Subgraph Explor
 ations</b>&nbsp\;<a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2102.05152">https://arxiv.o
 rg/abs/2102.05152</a>&nbsp\;(<a href="https://arxiv.org/search/cs?searchtyp
 e=author&amp\;query=Yuan%2C+H">Hao Yuan</a>\,&nbsp\;<a href="https://arxiv.
 org/search/cs?searchtype=author&amp\;query=Yu%2C+H">Haiyang Yu</a>\,&nbsp\;
 <a href="https://arxiv.org/search/cs?searchtype=author&amp\;query=Wang%2C+J
 ">Jie Wang</a>\,&nbsp\;<a href="https://arxiv.org/search/cs?searchtype=auth
 or&amp\;query=Li%2C+K">Kang Li</a>\,&nbsp\;<a href="https://arxiv.org/searc
 h/cs?searchtype=author&amp\;query=Ji%2C+S">Shuiwang Ji</a>)<br>We consider 
 the problem of explaining the predictions of graph neural networks (GNNs)\,
  which otherwise are considered as black boxes. Existing methods invariably
  focus on explaining the importance of graph nodes or edges but ignore the 
 substructures of graphs\, which are more intuitive and human-intelligible. 
 In this work\, we propose a novel method\, known as SubgraphX\, to explain 
 GNNs by identifying important subgraphs. Given a trained GNN model and an i
 nput graph\, our SubgraphX explains its predictions by efficiently explorin
 g different subgraphs with Monte Carlo tree search. To make the tree search
  more effective\, we propose to use Shapley values as a measure of subgraph
  importance\, which can also capture the interactions among different subgr
 aphs. To expedite computations\, we propose efficient approximation schemes
  to compute Shapley values for graph data. Our work represents the first at
 tempt to explain GNNs via identifying subgraphs explicitly and directly. Ex
 perimental results show that our SubgraphX achieves significantly improved 
 explanations\, while keeping computations at a reasonable level.<br><b><br>
 </b><b>Meeting Details:<br></b><i>Every Tuesday at 5pm CEST / 3pm UTC / 11a
 m EST / 8am PST</i><br><a href="https://us02web.zoom.us/j/4556095405?pwd=Um
 ZRbC95d050TkRsZ2QvWER3SDdqUT09">https://us02web.zoom.us/j/4556095405?pwd=Um
 ZRbC95d050TkRsZ2QvWER3SDdqUT09</a><br>Meeting ID: 455 609 5405<br>Passcode:
  logag<br><br><b>Join Slack Workspace for discussions beyond the meetings a
 nd paper voting:<br></b><a href="https://join.slack.com/t/logag/shared_invi
 te/zt-u0mbo1ec-zElmvd1oSCXGjXvxLSokvg">https://join.slack.com/t/<u></u>loga
