DTSTART;TZID=Europe/Berlin:20240429T170000
DTEND;TZID=Europe/Berlin:20240429T180000
DTSTAMP:20260325T005103Z
ORGANIZER;CN=vdujn2pbpkhgr5f55cm39qbmu8@group.calendar.google.com:mailto:vd
 ujn2pbpkhgr5f55cm39qbmu8@group.calendar.google.com
UID:4om8dkr7eu2gvvk4o4mi4c9bjg_R20231211T160000@google.com
ATTENDEE;CUTYPE=INDIVIDUAL;ROLE=REQ-PARTICIPANT;PARTSTAT=NEEDS-ACTION;CN=fa
 rhank@mit.edu;X-NUM-GUESTS=0:mailto:farhank@mit.edu
RECURRENCE-ID;TZID=Europe/Berlin:20240429T170000
CREATED:20210724T162031Z
DESCRIPTION:<u></u><u></u><u></u><u></u><u></u><b>Speaker:<br></b>Farhan Kh
 odaee is a PhD candidate in the Mechanical Engineering Department at MIT ad
 vised by Elazer Edelman and Jonathan Weissman. His research aims to use eng
 ineering methods and new high-content biological measurements to understand
  the emergence of life-bearing systems at a more fundamental level. Previou
 sly\, he obtained an M.Sc in Biomedical Engineering from the University of 
 Denver\, Colorado\, where his thesis focused on the development of innovati
 ve transcatheter heart valve technologies.<br><br><b>Paper:</b><br><b>Multi
 modal language models for mapping the genotype-phenotype relationship</b><b
 r>How complex phenotypes emerge from intricate gene expression patterns is 
 a fundamental question in biology. Quantitative characterization of this re
 lationship\, however\, is challenging due to the vast combinatorial possibi
 lities and dynamic interplay between genotype and phenotype landscapes. Int
 egrating high-content genotyping approaches such as single-cell RNA sequenc
 ing and advanced learning methods such as language models offers an opportu
 nity for dissecting this complex relationship. Here\, I present an integrat
 ed genetics framework to analyze and interpret the high-dimensional landsca
 pe of genotypes and their associated phenotypes simultaneously. We applied 
 this approach to develop a multimodal foundation model to explore the genot
 ype-phenotype relationship manifold for human transcriptomics at the cellul
 ar level. The results show a refined resolution of cellular heterogeneity\,
  enhanced precision in phenotype annotating\, and uncovered potential cross
 -tissue biomarkers that are undetectable through conventional gene expressi
 on analysis alone. Utilizing contextualized embeddings\, we investigated ge
 ne polyfunctionality which illustrates the multifaceted roles that genes pl
 ay in different biological processes\, and show that for VWF gene in endoth
 elial cells. Overall\, this study aims to advance our understanding of the 
 dynamic interplay between gene expression and phenotypic manifestation and 
 demonstrates the potential of integrated genetics in uncovering new dimensi
 ons of cellular function and complexity.<b><br><br></b><b>Meeting Details:<
 br></b><i>Every Monday at</i><i> </i><i>5pm CST / 4pm UTC / 11am EST / 8am 
 PST.  </i><br><a href="https://zoom.us/j/5775722530?pwd=ZzlGTXlDNThhUDZOdU4
 vN2JRMm5pQT09" target="_blank">https://zoom.us/j/5775722530?pwd=ZzlGTXlDNTh
 hUDZOdU4vN2JRMm5pQT09</a><br><br><b>Slack Workspace for discussion and pape
 r voting:<br></b><a href="https://join.slack.com/t/logag/shared_invite/zt-2
 2y7n3k7a-FHwX31gc85yZCa0uF8BU7w" target="_blank">https://join.slack.com/t/l
 ogag/shared_invite/zt-22y7n3k7a-FHwX31gc85yZCa0uF8BU7w</a><br><br><b>All in
 formation: Schedule of upcoming papers\, recordings\, mailing list</b><b>:<
 br></b><u><a href="https://hannes-stark.com/logag-reading-group" target="_b
 lank">https://hannes-stark.com/logag-reading-group</a></u><b><br></b><u></u
 ><u></u><u></u><u></u><u></u>
LAST-MODIFIED:20250330T034736Z
