DTSTAMP:20260325T005103Z
ORGANIZER;CN=vdujn2pbpkhgr5f55cm39qbmu8@group.calendar.google.com:mailto:vd
 ujn2pbpkhgr5f55cm39qbmu8@group.calendar.google.com
UID:FEEAEC38-0494-443E-BE84-F231E30E5AF5
ATTENDEE;CUTYPE=INDIVIDUAL;ROLE=REQ-PARTICIPANT;PARTSTAT=ACCEPTED;CN=bchamb
 erlain@twitter.com;X-NUM-GUESTS=0:mailto:bchamberlain@twitter.com
ATTENDEE;CUTYPE=INDIVIDUAL;ROLE=REQ-PARTICIPANT;PARTSTAT=ACCEPTED;CN=fdigio
 vanni@twitter.com;X-NUM-GUESTS=0:mailto:fdigiovanni@twitter.com
ATTENDEE;CUTYPE=INDIVIDUAL;ROLE=REQ-PARTICIPANT;PARTSTAT=ACCEPTED;CN=jabrow
 bottom@gmail.com;X-NUM-GUESTS=0:mailto:jabrowbottom@gmail.com
ATTENDEE;CUTYPE=INDIVIDUAL;ROLE=REQ-PARTICIPANT;PARTSTAT=ACCEPTED;CN=jrowbo
 ttom@twitter.com;X-NUM-GUESTS=0:mailto:jrowbottom@twitter.com
ATTENDEE;CUTYPE=RESOURCE;ROLE=REQ-PARTICIPANT;PARTSTAT=DECLINED;CN="London-
 1-Raptor (6) [Google Meet, LCD, Phone]";X-NUM-GUESTS=0:mailto:twitter.com_2
 d323136353935393836@resource.calendar.google.com
RECURRENCE-ID;TZID=Europe/Berlin:20220830T170000
CREATED:20220820T195615Z
DESCRIPTION:<html-blob><u></u><u></u><u></u><b>Speaker:<br></b>Dr.&nbsp\;<a
  href="https://scholar.google.com/citations?user=yzjjeqsAAAAJ&hl=en">France
 sco Di Giovanni</a>&nbsp\;obtained his PhD from University College London a
 nd is working together with Michael Bronstein at Twitter Research.&nbsp\;<a
  href="https://www.linkedin.com/in/jamesrowbottom/">James Rowbottom</a>&nbs
 p\;received his master in AI with distinction from Imperial College London 
 and is also at Twitter.<br><br><b>Paper:</b><br><b>Graph Neural Networks as
  Gradient Flows&nbsp\;</b><a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2206.10991" class=
 "paper-link" style="display: inline-block\;">https://arxiv.org/abs/2206.109
 91</a> (<a href="https://arxiv.org/search/cs?searchtype=author&query=Di+Gio
 vanni%2C+F">Francesco Di Giovanni</a>\,&nbsp\;<a href="https://arxiv.org/se
 arch/cs?searchtype=author&query=Rowbottom%2C+J">James Rowbottom</a>\,&nbsp\
 ;<a href="https://arxiv.org/search/cs?searchtype=author&query=Chamberlain%2
 C+B+P">Benjamin P. Chamberlain</a>\,&nbsp\;<a href="https://arxiv.org/searc
 h/cs?searchtype=author&query=Markovich%2C+T">Thomas Markovich</a>\,&nbsp\;<
 a href="https://arxiv.org/search/cs?searchtype=author&query=Bronstein%2C+M+
 M">Michael M. Bronstein</a>)<br>Dynamical systems minimizing an energy are 
 ubiquitous in geometry and physics. We propose a gradient flow framework fo
 r GNNs where the equations follow the direction of steepest descent of a le
 arnable energy. This approach allows to explain the GNN evolution from a mu
 lti-particle perspective as learning attractive and repulsive forces in fea
 ture space via the positive and negative eigenvalues of a symmetric "channe
 l-mixing" matrix. We perform spectral analysis of the solutions and conclud
 e that gradient flow graph convolutional models can induce a dynamics domin
 ated by the graph high frequencies which is desirable for heterophilic data
 sets. We also describe structural constraints on common GNN architectures a
 llowing to interpret them as gradient flows. We perform thorough ablation s
 tudies corroborating our theoretical analysis and show competitive performa
 nce of simple and lightweight models on real-world homophilic and heterophi
 lic datasets.<br><b><br></b><b>Meeting Details:<br></b><i>Every Tuesday at<
 /i><i> </i><i>5pm CEST / 3pm UTC / 11am EST / 8am PDT.&nbsp\;&nbsp\;</i><br
 ><a href="https://zoom.us/j/5775722530?pwd=ZzlGTXlDNThhUDZOdU4vN2JRMm5pQT09
 ">https://zoom.us/j/5775722530?pwd=ZzlGTXlDNThhUDZOdU4vN2JRMm5pQT09</a><br>
 <br><b>Slack Workspace for discussion and paper voting:<br></b><a href="htt
 ps://join.slack.com/t/logag/shared_invite/zt-u0mbo1ec-zElmvd1oSCXGjXvxLSokv
 g">https://join.slack.com/t/<u></u>logag/shared_invite/zt-<u></u>u0mbo1ec-<
