DTSTART;TZID=Europe/Berlin:20220823T170000
DTEND;TZID=Europe/Berlin:20220823T180000
DTSTAMP:20260325T005103Z
ORGANIZER;CN=vdujn2pbpkhgr5f55cm39qbmu8@group.calendar.google.com:mailto:vd
 ujn2pbpkhgr5f55cm39qbmu8@group.calendar.google.com
UID:FEEAEC38-0494-443E-BE84-F231E30E5AF5
ATTENDEE;CUTYPE=INDIVIDUAL;ROLE=REQ-PARTICIPANT;PARTSTAT=ACCEPTED;CN=omri.p
 uny@weizmann.ac.il;X-NUM-GUESTS=0:mailto:omri.puny@weizmann.ac.il
RECURRENCE-ID;TZID=Europe/Berlin:20220823T170000
CREATED:20220820T195614Z
DESCRIPTION:<html-blob><u></u><u></u><b>Speaker:<br></b>First author <a hre
 f="https://omri1348.github.io/" id="ow611" __is_owner="true">Omri Puny</a> 
 who is a PhD student in the Department of Computer Science and Applied Math
 ematics at the Weizmann Institute of Science under the supervision of&nbsp\
 ;<a href="https://www.wisdom.weizmann.ac.il/~ylipman/">Prof. Yaron Lipman</
 a>. My research focuses on developing deep learning models for iregular dat
 a\, mainly graphs and point clouds.<br><br><b>Paper:</b><br>Frame Averaging
  for Invariant and Equivariant Network Design&nbsp\;https://arxiv.org/abs/2
 110.03336 (<a href="https://arxiv.org/search/cs?searchtype=author&amp\;quer
 y=Puny%2C+O" class="paper-link" style="display: inline-block\;">Omri Puny</
 a>\,&nbsp\;<a href="https://arxiv.org/search/cs?searchtype=author&amp\;quer
 y=Atzmon%2C+M" class="paper-link" style="display: inline-block\;">Matan Atz
 mon</a>\,&nbsp\;<a href="https://arxiv.org/search/cs?searchtype=author&amp\
 ;query=Ben-Hamu%2C+H" class="paper-link" style="display: inline-block\;">He
 li Ben-Hamu</a>\,&nbsp\;<a href="https://arxiv.org/search/cs?searchtype=aut
 hor&amp\;query=Misra%2C+I" class="paper-link" style="display: inline-block\
 ;">Ishan Misra</a>\,&nbsp\;<a href="https://arxiv.org/search/cs?searchtype=
 author&amp\;query=Grover%2C+A" class="paper-link" style="display: inline-bl
 ock\;">Aditya Grover</a>\,&nbsp\;<a href="https://arxiv.org/search/cs?searc
 htype=author&amp\;query=Smith%2C+E+J" class="paper-link" style="display: in
 line-block\;">Edward J. Smith</a>\,&nbsp\;<a href="https://arxiv.org/search
 /cs?searchtype=author&amp\;query=Lipman%2C+Y" class="paper-link" style="dis
 play: inline-block\;">Yaron Lipman</a>)<br>Many machine learning tasks invo
 lve learning functions that are known to be invariant or equivariant to cer
 tain symmetries of the input data. However\, it is often challenging to des
 ign neural network architectures that respect these symmetries while being 
 expressive and computationally efficient. For example\, Euclidean motion in
 variant/equivariant graph or point cloud neural networks. We introduce Fram
 e Averaging (FA)\, a general purpose and systematic framework for adapting 
 known (backbone) architectures to become invariant or equivariant to new sy
 mmetry types. Our framework builds on the well known group averaging operat
 or that guarantees invariance or equivariance but is intractable. In contra
 st\, we observe that for many important classes of symmetries\, this operat
 or can be replaced with an averaging operator over a small subset of the gr
 oup elements\, called a frame. We show that averaging over a frame guarante
 es exact invariance or equivariance while often being much simpler to compu
 te than averaging over the entire group. Furthermore\, we prove that FA-bas
 ed models have maximal expressive power in a broad setting and in general p
 reserve the expressive power of their backbone architectures. Using frame a
 veraging\, we propose a new class of universal Graph Neural Networks (GNNs)
 \, universal Euclidean motion invariant point cloud networks\, and Euclidea
 n motion invariant Message Passing (MP) GNNs. We demonstrate the practical 
 effectiveness of FA on several applications including point cloud normal es
 timation\, beyond&nbsp\;2-WL graph separation\, and&nbsp\;n-body dynamics p
 rediction\, achieving state-of-the-art results in all of these benchmarks.<
 br><b><br></b><b>Meeting Details:<br></b><i>Every Tuesday at</i><i> </i><i>
 5pm CEST / 3pm UTC / 11am EST / 8am PDT.&nbsp\;&nbsp\;</i><br><a href="http
 s://zoom.us/j/5775722530?pwd=ZzlGTXlDNThhUDZOdU4vN2JRMm5pQT09">https://zoom
 .us/j/5775722530?pwd=ZzlGTXlDNThhUDZOdU4vN2JRMm5pQT09</a><br><br><b>Slack W
 orkspace for discussion and paper voting:<br></b><a href="https://join.slac
 k.com/t/logag/shared_invite/zt-u0mbo1ec-zElmvd1oSCXGjXvxLSokvg">https://joi
 n.slack.com/t/<u></u>logag/shared_invite/zt-<u></u>u0mbo1ec-<u></u>zElmvd1o
 SCXGjXvxLSokvg</a><br><br><b>All information: Schedule of upcoming papers\,
  recordings\, mailing list</b><b>:<br></b><u><a href="https://hannes-stark.
 com/logag-reading-group">https://hannes-stark.com/logag-reading-group</a></
 u><b><br></b><u></u><u></u></html-blob>
LAST-MODIFIED:20250330T034607Z
