ATTENDEE;CUTYPE=INDIVIDUAL;ROLE=REQ-PARTICIPANT;PARTSTAT=NEEDS-ACTION;CN=bz
 ho3923@uni.sydney.edu.au;X-NUM-GUESTS=0:mailto:bzho3923@uni.sydney.edu.au
ATTENDEE;CUTYPE=INDIVIDUAL;ROLE=REQ-PARTICIPANT;PARTSTAT=NEEDS-ACTION;CN=ka
 i.yi@unsw.edu.au;X-NUM-GUESTS=0:mailto:kai.yi@unsw.edu.au
ATTENDEE;CUTYPE=INDIVIDUAL;ROLE=REQ-PARTICIPANT;PARTSTAT=NEEDS-ACTION;CN=yu
 guang.wang@sjtu.edu.cn;X-NUM-GUESTS=0:mailto:yuguang.wang@sjtu.edu.cn
RECURRENCE-ID;TZID=Europe/Berlin:20230814T170000
CREATED:20210724T162031Z
DESCRIPTION:<u></u><u></u><u></u><u></u><u></u><b>Speakers:<br></b><b>Bingx
 in Zhou</b> is currently a Research Scientist at Shanghai Jiao Tong Univers
 ity. She obtained her Ph.D. degree at the University of Sydney\, Australia 
 in 2022. Her present research primarily focuses on the development of spect
 ral graph neural networks (GNNs)\, along with utilizing GNNs to address str
 uctural biology challenges in the fields such as enzyme engineering and ant
 ibody design. She has developed several useful GNNs based on undecimated an
 d decimated Framelet-based graph convolutions that have been proven useful 
 for static\, dynamic\, and noisy graphs. For protein designs\, she has made
  contributions to the field by establishing various efficient geometric met
 hodologies for directed evolution and de novo protein design. These strateg
 ies have demonstrated their efficacy via in silico evaluation criteria and 
 have also shown promising results under laboratory conditions.<br><b>Kai Yi
 </b> is a third-year Ph.D. student majoring in statistics at the School of 
 Mathematics and Statistics in the University of New South Wales (UNSW) in S
 ydney\, Australia. His research interests lie in geometric deep learning an
 d Bayesian inference and application on real world data.<br><br><b>Paper:</
 b><br><b>Graph Denoising Diffusion for Inverse Protein Folding </b><a href=
 "https://arxiv.org/abs/2306.16819">https://arxiv.org/abs/2306.16819</a> (<a
  href="https://arxiv.org/search/q-bio?searchtype=author&amp\;query=Yi%2C+K"
 ><u>Kai Yi</u></a>\, <a href="https://arxiv.org/search/q-bio?searchtype=aut
 hor&amp\;query=Zhou%2C+B">Bingxin Zhou</a>\, <a href="https://arxiv.org/sea
 rch/q-bio?searchtype=author&amp\;query=Shen%2C+Y">Yiqing Shen</a>\, <a href
 ="https://arxiv.org/search/q-bio?searchtype=author&amp\;query=Li%C3%B2%2C+P
 ">Pietro Liò</a>\, <a href="https://arxiv.org/search/q-bio?searchtype=autho
 r&amp\;query=Wang%2C+Y+G">Yu Guang Wang</a>)<br>Inverse protein folding is 
 challenging due to its inherent one-to-many mapping characteristic\, where 
 numerous possible amino acid sequences can fold into a single\, identical p
 rotein backbone. This task involves not only identifying viable sequences b
 ut also representing the sheer diversity of potential solutions. However\, 
 existing discriminative models\, such as transformer-based auto-regressive 
 models\, struggle to encapsulate the diverse range of plausible solutions. 
 In contrast\, diffusion probabilistic models\, as an emerging genre of gene
 rative approaches\, offer the potential to generate a diverse set of sequen
 ce candidates for determined protein backbones. We propose a novel graph de
 noising diffusion model for inverse protein folding\, where a given protein
  backbone guides the diffusion process on the corresponding amino acid resi
 due types. The model infers the joint distribution of amino acids condition
 ed on the nodes' physiochemical properties and local environment. Moreover\
 , we utilize amino acid replacement matrices for the diffusion forward proc
 ess\, encoding the biologically-meaningful prior knowledge of amino acids f
 rom their spatial and sequential neighbors as well as themselves\, which re
 duces the sampling space of the generative process. Our model achieves stat
 e-of-the-art performance over a set of popular baseline methods in sequence
  recovery and exhibits great potential in generating diverse protein sequen
 ces for a determined protein backbone structure.<br><b><br> <br></b><b>Meet
 ing Details:<br></b><i>Every Monday at</i><i> </i><i>5pm CEST / 3pm UTC / 1
 1am EDT / 8am PDT.  </i><br><a href="https://zoom.us/j/5775722530?pwd=ZzlGT
 XlDNThhUDZOdU4vN2JRMm5pQT09">https://zoom.us/j/5775722530?pwd=ZzlGTXlDNThhU
 DZOdU4vN2JRMm5pQT09</a><br><br><b>Slack Workspace for discussion and paper 
 voting:<br></b><a href="https://join.slack.com/t/logag/shared_invite/zt-1hc
 qybjyd-ty5Pbj~BQy~W4B4mQQVxDQ">https://join.slack.com/t/logag/shared_invite
 /zt-1hcqybjyd-ty5Pbj~BQy~W4B4mQQVxDQ</a><br><br><b>All information: Schedul
 e of upcoming papers\, recordings\, mailing list</b><b>:<br></b><u><a href=
 "https://hannes-stark.com/logag-reading-group">https://hannes-stark.com/log
 ag-reading-group</a></u><b><br></b><u></u><u></u><u></u><u></u><u></u>
LAST-MODIFIED:20240904T130202Z
