ATTENDEE;CUTYPE=INDIVIDUAL;ROLE=REQ-PARTICIPANT;PARTSTAT=NEEDS-ACTION;CN=fd
 405@cam.ac.uk;X-NUM-GUESTS=0:mailto:fd405@cam.ac.uk
ATTENDEE;CUTYPE=INDIVIDUAL;ROLE=REQ-PARTICIPANT;PARTSTAT=NEEDS-ACTION;CN=ko
 nstantin.rusch@sam.math.ethz.ch;X-NUM-GUESTS=0:mailto:konstantin.rusch@sam.
 math.ethz.ch
RECURRENCE-ID;TZID=Europe/Berlin:20230821T170000
CREATED:20210724T162031Z
DESCRIPTION:<u></u><u></u><u></u><u></u><u></u><b>Speaker:<br></b>The autho
 rs Francesco Di Giovanni and Konstantin Rusch.<br><br><b>Paper:</b><br><b>H
 ow does over-squashing affect the power of GNNs? </b><a href="https://arxiv
 .org/abs/2306.03589">https://arxiv.org/abs/2306.03589</a> (<a href="https:/
 /arxiv.org/search/cs?searchtype=author&amp\;query=Di+Giovanni%2C+F">Frances
 co Di Giovanni</a>\, <a href="https://arxiv.org/search/cs?searchtype=author
 &amp\;query=Rusch%2C+T+K">T. Konstantin Rusch</a>\, <a href="https://arxiv.
 org/search/cs?searchtype=author&amp\;query=Bronstein%2C+M+M">Michael M. Bro
 nstein</a>\, <a href="https://arxiv.org/search/cs?searchtype=author&amp\;qu
 ery=Deac%2C+A">Andreea Deac</a>\, <a href="https://arxiv.org/search/cs?sear
 chtype=author&amp\;query=Lackenby%2C+M">Marc Lackenby</a>\, <a href="https:
 //arxiv.org/search/cs?searchtype=author&amp\;query=Mishra%2C+S">Siddhartha 
 Mishra</a>\, <a href="https://arxiv.org/search/cs?searchtype=author&amp\;qu
 ery=Veli%C4%8Dkovi%C4%87%2C+P">Petar Veličković</a>)<br>Graph Neural Networ
 ks (GNNs) are the state-of-the-art model for machine learning on graph-stru
 ctured data. The most popular class of GNNs operate by exchanging informati
 on between adjacent nodes\, and are known as Message Passing Neural Network
 s (MPNNs). Given their widespread use\, understanding the expressive power 
 of MPNNs is a key question. However\, existing results typically consider s
 ettings with uninformative node features. In this paper\, we provide a rigo
 rous analysis to determine which function classes of node features can be l
 earned by an MPNN of a given capacity. We do so by measuring the level of p
 airwise interactions between nodes that MPNNs allow for. This measure provi
 des a novel quantitative characterization of the so-called over-squashing e
 ffect\, which is observed to occur when a large volume of messages is aggre
 gated into fixed-size vectors. Using our measure\, we prove that\, to guara
 ntee sufficient communication between pairs of nodes\, the capacity of the 
 MPNN must be large enough\, depending on properties of the input graph stru
 cture\, such as commute times. For many relevant scenarios\, our analysis r
 esults in impossibility statements in practice\, showing that over-squashin
 g hinders the expressive power of MPNNs. We validate our theoretical findin
 gs through extensive controlled experiments and ablation studies.<br><b><br
 ></b><br><b>Meeting Details:<br></b><i>Every Monday at</i><i> </i><i>5pm CE
 ST / 3pm UTC / 11am EDT / 8am PDT.  </i><br><a href="https://zoom.us/j/5775
 722530?pwd=ZzlGTXlDNThhUDZOdU4vN2JRMm5pQT09">https://zoom.us/j/5775722530?p
 wd=ZzlGTXlDNThhUDZOdU4vN2JRMm5pQT09</a><br><br><b>Slack Workspace for discu
 ssion and paper voting:<br></b><a href="https://join.slack.com/t/logag/shar
 ed_invite/zt-1hcqybjyd-ty5Pbj~BQy~W4B4mQQVxDQ">https://join.slack.com/t/log
 ag/shared_invite/zt-1hcqybjyd-ty5Pbj~BQy~W4B4mQQVxDQ</a><br><br><b>All info
 rmation: Schedule of upcoming papers\, recordings\, mailing list</b><b>:<br
 ></b><u><a href="https://hannes-stark.com/logag-reading-group">https://hann
 es-stark.com/logag-reading-group</a></u><b><br></b><u></u><u></u><u></u><u>
 </u><u></u>
